00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:30.444
to make sure everything's good.

00:03:30.444 --> 00:03:31.620
So again, thank you for this opportunity.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.900
I'm Cindy Nguyen, a cultural
historian of Vietnam libraries

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.860
and print culture.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:38.900
Today's talk, I will first discuss

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.627
the main research
questions and interventions

00:03:41.627 --> 00:03:43.450
of my book manuscript titled "Misreading."

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:45.886
I'll share an excerpt from the book,

00:03:45.886 --> 00:03:47.930
focus on the case study
of the 1930s to 1940s,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.910
Book Wagons Bibliobus project

00:03:50.808 --> 00:03:52.100
in Cochinchina, southern Vietnam.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.451
And I'll conclude with a discussion

00:03:54.451 --> 00:03:56.552
on the book's chapters,
scope, and major contributions

00:03:56.552 --> 00:03:57.730
to the field.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:58.790
So my book project title "Misreading,"

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.280
examines the cultural and
political history of libraries

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.880
and print control in
Vietnam from 1865 to 1958.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.191
I analyze the changing
mission of colonial libraries

00:04:09.191 --> 00:04:10.430
as a hybrid of state documentation

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:13.140
and a public space for
self-directed education

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.890
and social life.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:19.330
Focused primarily on the
state Central Library in Hanoi

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:22.433
and the Cochinchina Library in Saigon

00:04:22.433 --> 00:04:23.266
shown here in this slide.

00:04:23.266 --> 00:04:25.310
This book advances a two-part argument

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:28.940
on the history of libraries
and print control in Indochina

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.690
which today consists of
Vietnam, Cambodia, Laos.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.053
First to build libraries
is to build the state.

00:04:35.654 --> 00:04:37.674
Libraries legitimize the
authority of the state

00:04:37.674 --> 00:04:38.880
as infrastructures of symbolic modernity,

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.590
print control and documentary heritage.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:45.343
Second, library users also
shape the everyday mission

00:04:45.343 --> 00:04:46.310
and social function of the library

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.300
beyond these hegemonic aspirations

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.340
of colonial and post-colonial states.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.900
This study demonstrates
how builders and users

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.060
articulated a meaning of public

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.290
through the functions of
the colonial state libraries

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.960
to serve its readers, to
preserve documentary heritage,

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.010
and to operate as a broader instrument

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.960
of public education and moralization.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.330
This two-part argument
reveals the significance

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.060
of the library as an
institution of state-building,

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.453
print control and public reading culture.

00:05:15.867 --> 00:05:18.960
This book is the first
comprehensive history

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.290
of the library in Southeast Asia.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.170
The narrative is book-ended
by two formative events,

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.870
the founding of the Cochinchina
French colonial library

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:30.800
in 1865, which was the first
colonial state public library

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.360
in Indochina and the centralization
of the National Library

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:38.890
of the new Republic of Vietnam
in 1958, South Vietnam.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:39.850
So this chronology

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:41.880
challenges externally-defined
chronologies,

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.410
such as 1945 World War II,

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.020
1954, the end of the first Indochina war,

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:49.890
and completely offers this
new frame of reference

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.340
in understanding into
processes of colonization

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.740
and decolonization

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:56.645
and creation of
post-colonial nation States

00:05:56.645 --> 00:05:58.190
from the perspective of Vietnam.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:01.070
In terms of source material,
I primarily used newspapers,

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:04.520
official archives, reader
statistics in Vietnam and France.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.480
My top-down and bottom-up
analysis of library builders

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.830
and users contributes to the findings

00:06:10.882 --> 00:06:13.700
of the colonial state libraries,
public and urban space,

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:15.260
knowledge, curation, and print culture.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.620
With attention to labor, access and space,

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:21.022
the book sheds sight on the
politics of gender and race

00:06:21.022 --> 00:06:22.480
in libraries, as well as its legacies

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.250
in post-colonial nation
building in Vietnam.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.940
So as a way to illuminate the arguments

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.262
of the book manuscript, I will focus

00:06:30.262 --> 00:06:31.660
on the specific case study
of the mobile library

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.420
wagon project in
Cochinchina, South Vietnam

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:35.943
in 1930s to 1940s.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:42.730
On March, 1936, the first Bibliobus

00:06:43.765 --> 00:06:46.290
or mobile library book wagon in Indochina

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:49.560
began its journey through
nine provinces in Cochinchina.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:53.805
The library wagons circulated 832 French

00:06:53.805 --> 00:06:55.260
and Vietnamese books
of popular literature,

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.110
politics, morality, and science

00:06:57.985 --> 00:06:59.440
from the Cochinchina's Saigon library.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:01.170
Every two months the book wagon toured

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:02.630
through the nine provinces,

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.730
lending books to Vietnamese
and French readers.

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:06.810
A Vietnamese driver and secretary

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:09.920
from the Cochinchina library
accompanied the wagon.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.280
The book wagon encountered
many delays and accidents.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.340
For example, on September 1st, 1937,

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:17.200
on the way from Bà Rịa to Biên Hòa

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.000
the book wagon struck a tree stump

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.600
when swerving to avoid
hitting a seven-year-old girl

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.040
who was in the road.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.450
The accident severely damaged the wagon,

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:28.030
delaying the trip for one month

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:31.000
while the vehicle underwent
extensive repairs.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.130
The Bibliobus book wagon
continued its route

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:36.300
on November 15th, 1937 from Saigon

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.967
through the western Mekong Delta region

00:07:38.967 --> 00:07:40.013
following this schedule.

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:42.350
So in this schedule here,

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:44.900
you see that this is the
first mobile book wagon,

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.210
after its repairs departing from Saigon.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:50.220
Note the limited operating
hours of the mobile wagon

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.020
at each stop as well the central location

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:56.640
in front of administrative
offices, schools and hospitals.

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:57.730
Over the course of the year,

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:01.390
the mobile book wagon served
a total of 10,000 readers.

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:03.570
Due to the success of
the first book circuit

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.730
on December, 1937 a
second mobile book wagon

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.630
traveled through eight other provinces

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:14.839
of Cochinchina, (listing foreign names).

00:08:18.204 --> 00:08:21.530
The second mobile wagon served
a total of 21,500 readers

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.590
over the course of one year.

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:27.005
The story of the mobile book
wagons seems particularly

00:08:27.005 --> 00:08:27.900
ambitious and curious.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:31.228
Why did the governmental and
public Cochinchina library

00:08:31.228 --> 00:08:33.410
circulate its valuable
collections to the countryside?

00:08:33.410 --> 00:08:35.830
What books moved from the
urban center of Saigon

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:37.170
to the provinces?

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.670
Who were the readers and
what did they check out?

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:43.070
A closer examination of this
peculiar project sheds light

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.390
on the geographic and symbolic extension

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.850
of the colonial state into the countryside

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:49.843
through physically bringing

00:08:49.843 --> 00:08:51.240
quote good reading to the people.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.050
Good reading, I argue was
a changing political moral

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.410
and literary concept
advanced by the French

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.130
and Vietnamese government administrators,

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:03.210
librarians, publishers,
writers, as well as readers.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.470
This talk offers three discoveries.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.120
First it reveals how
high level administrators

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.100
in the Indochina library director advanced

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:14.410
a cultural propaganda project
to disseminate good reading

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:16.543
as didactic, politically safe,

00:09:17.545 --> 00:09:19.785
and vulgarizing reading
matter to the countryside

00:09:19.785 --> 00:09:20.618
through book wagons.

00:09:20.618 --> 00:09:22.425
Secondly, I show

00:09:22.425 --> 00:09:24.080
how this state initiated
book wagons project

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.710
intersected with the development

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:29.044
of Vietnamese reading
public actively enmeshed

00:09:29.044 --> 00:09:31.600
in global translated literature
in French and Vietnamese

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:34.385
as well as a growing body Quoc Ngu,

00:09:34.385 --> 00:09:36.710
a Romanized script Vietnamese literature.

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:40.143
Thirdly, I reveal a vibrant
Vietnamese public sphere

00:09:40.143 --> 00:09:41.800
who debated the role of good reading

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:44.220
and called for the development

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:45.920
of Quoc Ngu library collections

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.510
for the larger Vietnamese public.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:49.390
My primary research
question is the following:

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:51.390
What role did the official state library

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.410
such as the Cochinchina library

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:55.670
and then also the Hanoi Central Library

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:58.766
play within this larger
landscape of print culture

00:09:58.766 --> 00:10:00.930
and reading public in
the late colonial period?

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:03.830
This talk on the Bibliobus
mobile wagon project,

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:05.760
as well as the larger book manuscript

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.065
points to the mechanics,

00:10:08.065 --> 00:10:09.650
the discourse in everyday
practice of the library

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:13.110
to fulfill its role as an
official government institution,

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:15.230
a resource of public education,

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:16.860
and a cultural space for the practice

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:19.770
of collective responsibility,
urban civility

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:21.163
and public reading.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.360
From 1928 to 1930, the French
colonial administration

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.570
drew inspiration from the existing

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:32.086
Balai Pustaka state
sponsored publishing bureau,

00:10:32.086 --> 00:10:33.480
distribution office and library system

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:36.780
in the Dutch East Indies, today Indonesia.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:39.530
Governor General of
Indochina, Pierre Pasquier,

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:41.030
commissioned extensive studies

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:43.807
on the Dutch East Indies publishing

00:10:43.807 --> 00:10:44.968
and popular libraries projects,

00:10:44.968 --> 00:10:47.480
seeking to implement a similar
project in French Indochina.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.480
Emile Vayrac, the Director of the Office

00:10:50.326 --> 00:10:52.140
of Indigenous Publishing
and the Resident Superior

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.050
at the resident superior
of Tonkin, North Vietnam.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:58.325
He studied the Balai Pustaka in Indonesia

00:10:58.325 --> 00:11:00.630
and evaluated the current
state of publishing and print

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:01.630
in Indochina.

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:03.560
Vayrac argued that
beginning these publications

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:07.170
perpetuated dangerous criticisms
of French colonization

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:08.450
and civilization.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:11.317
He quotes, "Vietnamese literary
production is struggling

00:11:11.317 --> 00:11:14.777
"in anarchy and drags
itself into insignificance.

00:11:14.777 --> 00:11:17.057
"Apart from some
republications of old poems

00:11:17.057 --> 00:11:20.077
"or ancient moral guides,
small number of serious works,

00:11:20.077 --> 00:11:22.670
"the majority of Vietnamese
print criticizes us,"

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:25.920
the French, "and speaks
poorly of our civilization."

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.100
Praising the Dutch East
Indies, Vayrac argued

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:30.711
that the Balai Pustaka created libraries

00:11:30.711 --> 00:11:32.200
and schools, police stations, hospitals,

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:34.000
and instituted an office of publishing,

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.870
which produced and
distributed good reading

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:38.110
throughout the country.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:40.720
Colonial administrators, such
as Vayrac were keenly aware

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:42.890
and threatened by the rise and influence

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:46.309
of anti-colonial sentiment
and political organizing

00:11:46.309 --> 00:11:48.230
in Indochina in the 20's and the 30's.

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:49.926
In his essay,

00:11:49.926 --> 00:11:51.550
the "Program for the Recovery
of the Public Spirit,"

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.430
from his report, Vayrac elaborated

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.500
on the colonial administration's anxieties

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.053
over Vietnamese radicalism.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.890
Vayrac described the existing threat

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:02.190
of rapid revolutionary political ideas,

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.690
Chinese influence, anti-French sentiment

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:06.928
among a growing generation

00:12:06.928 --> 00:12:08.520
of dangerous, young
urban Vietnamese radicals

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.073
eager for change, as seen in this slide.

00:12:12.147 --> 00:12:13.640
Vayrac called attention
to the pervasiveness

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:16.160
of Bolshevik and anti-French agitation

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.400
amongst urban Vietnamese

00:12:18.423 --> 00:12:19.610
especially students, workers, youth.

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:22.740
The spirit of subversion
undermined the Vietnamese family,

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:24.840
corrupted the minds of young students,

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:28.440
and could potentially
permeate into the countryside.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.773
Within this context of
increased radicalism,

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.240
the colonial administration
developed new strategies

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:37.480
to control the quickly spreading
evil of insubordination

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:39.960
amongst outspoken Vietnamese.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:41.720
Vayrac believe that carefully crafted

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:43.650
and widespread propaganda messages

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:46.490
could counter the rising
political anti-French

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:49.390
and revolutionary sentiment
in the Vietnamese urban youth.

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:50.530
As seen in this slide,

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:54.080
Vayrac created this
indirect propaganda strategy

00:12:54.927 --> 00:12:57.170
where he emphasized the importance
of the rural population,

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:59.130
the group that he considered

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:01.150
not only the humble,
true people of Indochina,

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:03.160
but also the population most vulnerable

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:04.590
to the political ideologies

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:06.623
propagated by urban intellectuals.

00:13:07.634 --> 00:13:08.980
Vayrac suggested two effective methods

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:11.047
of indirect propaganda,

00:13:11.047 --> 00:13:11.880
propaganda through action

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:14.270
and propaganda by imagery and appearance.

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:16.260
This essay concludes with an attached list

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:19.546
of European works to
translate to Vietnamese.

00:13:19.546 --> 00:13:22.130
Vayrac argued that the
best works to translate

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:23.230
for Vietnamese readers

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:25.547
should include French popular literature

00:13:25.547 --> 00:13:26.990
from the middle ages such
as tales of Perrault,

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:29.826
La Fontaine's fables, Renard's novels,

00:13:29.826 --> 00:13:33.250
moral and educational
works as well as Proverbs.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:34.840
This list focused primarily

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.390
on instructional
apolitical texts he called

00:13:38.422 --> 00:13:41.280
that could civilize and morally
uplift Vietnamese readers.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:44.664
The colonial state saw
direct political interest

00:13:44.664 --> 00:13:45.980
in indigenous publishing and libraries

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:49.150
to produce and disseminate
a pro-colonial message.

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:50.960
Vayrac's study is only one of the examples

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:54.200
of extensive documentation from
the colonial administration

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:56.420
that show how control of
publishing and libraries

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:58.828
was essential as a propaganda tool

00:13:58.828 --> 00:14:00.020
for the colonialist state.

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:04.180
At the same time of this
process with Vayrac,

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:07.260
the Director of Archives
and Libraries, Paul Boudet

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.890
initiated efforts to create
provincial libraries,

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:12.946
a distribution network and
book wagons or Bibliobus,

00:14:12.946 --> 00:14:15.210
through the existing infrastructure

00:14:16.092 --> 00:14:19.210
of the state directorate library
system in Hanoi and Saigon.

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:21.180
I'll now discuss a bit
of contextual background

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:23.325
on the library system in Indochina

00:14:23.325 --> 00:14:24.600
and the role of Paul Boudet.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:27.230
The vision and organization
of the Indochina library

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:28.950
was deeply shaped

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:31.670
by the first and only colonial
era director Paul Boudet.

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:32.750
Paul Boudet graduated

00:14:33.646 --> 00:14:35.953
from the renowned archives
and library school in Chartres

00:14:35.953 --> 00:14:38.490
in 1909 as an archivist paleographer.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:41.560
On November 30th, 1917
Boudet was appointed

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:43.620
as Director of the newly centralized

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:47.223
Directorate of Archives
and Libraries in Indochina,

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:52.023
which, sorry, in Hanoi.

00:14:52.916 --> 00:14:54.076
So that was based in Hanoi.

00:14:54.076 --> 00:14:55.876
Paul Boudet was also the head director

00:14:55.876 --> 00:14:58.378
of the Hanoi central
library opened in 1919,

00:14:58.378 --> 00:14:59.750
which operated both as an extension

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:01.920
of the Bibliothèque nationale in France

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:03.730
and as an Indochina state library

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:06.260
in the colonial capital of Hanoi.

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:08.820
Paul Boudet standardized

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:10.510
technical and administrative policies

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:12.840
for documentation in IndoChina.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:15.400
Most importantly, Boudet
defined the function and mission

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:18.530
of a modern library based
off of French libraries.

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:20.510
He argued that encyclopedic libraries

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:23.830
must assemble the best works
of all levels of knowledge,

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:26.780
an orderly stable and
comprehensive collections.

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:29.160
A modern library followed
French model of libraries,

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:33.190
he argued specifically the BNF,
the Bibliothèque nationale,

00:15:33.190 --> 00:15:36.993
to provide scientifically
classified best works

00:15:36.993 --> 00:15:37.826
to the greater public.

00:15:37.826 --> 00:15:39.430
Boudet sought to implement these visions

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:41.860
to all the regional
libraries in Indochina,

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:44.500
the Hanoi Central Library, the
Cochinchina Central Library,

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:47.530
as well as the Phnom
Penh Cambodia library.

00:15:47.530 --> 00:15:49.680
Throughout the colonial period though,

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.730
the vision and reality
of the colonial library

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:53.570
as modern, encyclopedic, and public

00:15:54.576 --> 00:15:56.450
often differed in practice.

00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:57.450
Many of these libraries

00:15:57.450 --> 00:15:59.953
such as the Cochinchina library in Saigon

00:15:59.953 --> 00:16:01.851
originated first as a government library

00:16:01.851 --> 00:16:03.540
to provide documentation,
reference matter,

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:05.560
and reading leisure for
French administrators

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:08.750
and at times their wives
who had relocated to Saigon.

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.980
Yet by 1920s and 1930s,

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:14.376
an increasing number of
Vietnamese university students,

00:16:14.376 --> 00:16:15.600
commercial workers, and researchers

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.650
sought to access these
valuable library collections.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:20.640
Furthermore library administrators debated

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.996
what type of good reading should be added

00:16:22.996 --> 00:16:24.000
to the library collection.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.451
Libraries encountered budget constraints

00:16:26.451 --> 00:16:28.215
to build collections and hire staff.

00:16:28.215 --> 00:16:30.450
They also ran out, often
ran out of space for storage

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:33.596
and offered a limited number
of seats in the reading room

00:16:33.596 --> 00:16:35.910
as pictured here in the
Cochinchina library,

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:37.857
which was housed in the cramped

00:16:37.857 --> 00:16:39.836
government secretariat building together

00:16:39.836 --> 00:16:41.060
with the archives in Cochinchina.

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:43.170
In 1938, the reading
room held a collection

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:45.220
of 36,000 volumes.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.770
Although constrained by a
limited number of 44 seats,

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:51.140
the reading room was able
to welcome over 133 readers

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:52.270
each day.

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:54.750
During 1938, the popular lending section

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:59.493
recorded a total of 31,000
borrowers and 55,000 book loans.

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:02.463
Since the founding

00:17:02.463 --> 00:17:06.015
of the Directory of Archives
and Libraries in 1917,

00:17:06.015 --> 00:17:08.411
Boudet had envisioned an expansive network

00:17:08.411 --> 00:17:10.050
of provincial libraries
throughout Indochina.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:13.020
Inspired also by the Balai
Pustaka in Indonesia,

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:15.190
the Governor General Pierre Pasquier

00:17:15.190 --> 00:17:17.820
officially enlisted Boudet
to research public libraries

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:20.090
and book wagons around the world.

00:17:20.090 --> 00:17:23.657
On March 28th, 1929 Paul Boudet wrote

00:17:23.657 --> 00:17:25.690
to the American Library
Association office in Paris

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:27.577
and requested documents

00:17:27.577 --> 00:17:28.750
to study American circulating book wagons

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:29.860
and shipment service

00:17:31.016 --> 00:17:33.250
in order to develop a
similar project in Vietnam.

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:34.560
Boudet was particularly interested

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:37.610
in the practical cost efficient
methods used by automobiles

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:39.480
for the distribution of books.

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:41.590
From here begins an
extensive exchange of letters

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:44.480
between Boudet and libraries
from all around the world.

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:47.190
Boudet received extensive
replies and research resources

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:50.460
from the ALA, the American
Library Association in Paris,

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:53.410
the office in Chicago, even
the California State Library

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:54.770
in Sacramento.

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:57.310
American libraries shipped
hundreds of recent pamphlets,

00:17:57.310 --> 00:17:59.740
legal documents, book
catalogs and newspapers

00:18:00.975 --> 00:18:01.830
for Boudet to study the organization

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:04.613
of circulating libraries
and book wagons in America.

00:18:06.031 --> 00:18:07.050
These resources explore topics such

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:09.830
as the curatory
responsibility of librarians,

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:11.330
the importance of literacy

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:14.250
and access to useful
knowledge such as technology

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:16.230
or technical books.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:19.450
Boudet also wrote to the
Metropolitan Library in Beijing

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.280
in China, the Central
Library in Baroda, India

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:23.360
to request information

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:25.840
on their circulating libraries program.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:27.600
This points to the significant ways

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:29.490
in which models for library development

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:31.550
was a transnational process,

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:34.950
not always stemming from
metropole to colony.

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.010
From these comparative library models,

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:39.410
Boudet proposed his
circulating library project

00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:41.180
which consists of the following:

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:45.091
lending of French and Quốc
ngữ, a Romanized Vietnamese,

00:18:45.091 --> 00:18:46.600
a popular script of spoken Vietnamese,

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:48.190
books from the provinces,

00:18:48.190 --> 00:18:50.950
and the distribution of the
provinces, to the provinces

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:53.730
these books and pamphlets
published by indigenous writers

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:55.630
under official control.

00:18:55.630 --> 00:18:57.540
Recognizing the high cost of this project,

00:18:57.540 --> 00:18:59.848
Boudet emphasized the potential

00:18:59.848 --> 00:19:01.706
of a circulating library system

00:19:01.706 --> 00:19:03.120
to provide the European
indigenous population,

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:06.080
the provinces, a healthy leisure activity.

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:07.430
Additionally, Boudet argued

00:19:08.369 --> 00:19:10.110
that the circulating library
project combat the spread

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:12.470
of politically dangerous reading matter

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:14.518
among Vietnamese readers,

00:19:14.518 --> 00:19:17.060
such as works inspired by
Bolshevik propaganda pamphlets

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:20.273
widely distributed in China,
he says in this slide.

00:19:22.018 --> 00:19:22.851
Similar to Vayrac's proposal

00:19:22.851 --> 00:19:24.763
for counter propaganda initiatives,

00:19:25.716 --> 00:19:27.180
Boudet argued that circulating libraries

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:29.270
could also combat the dangerous influence

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:32.160
of Chinese revolutionary texts.

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:33.660
This counter propaganda strategy

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:37.100
sought to increase the
availability of safe texts

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:39.150
through state sponsored
distribution, libraries

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:41.849
and publishing in hopes that these texts

00:19:41.849 --> 00:19:43.280
would flood the Vietnamese print market.

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:45.500
By making safe reading
matter more plentiful,

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:48.120
colonial administrators
and Boudet hoped to compete

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:51.609
with the popularity of
illicit reading matter

00:19:51.609 --> 00:19:52.900
amongst Vietnamese.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.788
Throughout the 1930s, the Governor General

00:19:55.788 --> 00:19:58.673
and local administration
continued to discuss, debate,

00:19:58.673 --> 00:20:01.889
revise, and implement aspects
of the comprehensive project

00:20:01.889 --> 00:20:03.060
to develop popular libraries,

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:06.990
an office of publishing as
well as a book wagons project.

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:09.670
Yet an entirely centralized,
evenly distributed system

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:11.940
never materialized throughout Indochina

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:14.460
due to economic crises,
political insurrections,

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.580
and a rapid turnover in
political administration.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:19.930
Yet Boudet continued to
organize circulating book wagons

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:21.830
throughout the 30's and early 40's,

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:23.950
albeit with fiscal limitations.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:26.240
Here, for example, is a
photograph of a book wagon

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:28.868
circulating secular and Buddhist texts

00:20:28.868 --> 00:20:30.120
from the Royal Library in Cambodia

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:33.680
that was organized by another
scholar administrator,

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:37.060
Suzanne Karpelès, the curator
of the Buddhist Institute

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:40.410
and Royal Library in
Cambodge through the 1930s.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:42.020
This book wagon pictured on the left

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:43.590
circulated thousands of volumes

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:46.013
throughout the Southeast Cambodia,

00:20:46.994 --> 00:20:48.383
Southwest Cochinchina region.

00:20:49.374 --> 00:20:52.390
In comparison the Cambodge
Library pictured on the right

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:53.560
was functioned more as
the administrative archive

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:54.853
and reference library.

00:20:56.930 --> 00:20:59.089
The Directorate continued to organize

00:20:59.089 --> 00:21:00.740
these circulating library
services sporadically in the 40's,

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:04.333
especially throughout South
Vietnam, Cochinchina region.

00:21:04.333 --> 00:21:07.600
A 1946 report argued that
circulating libraries

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:10.090
brought French culture to
the outer isolated regions

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:13.720
of Cochinchina, which served
as a healthy leisure activity.

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:18.017
The report estimated
that throughout 36 to 46,

00:21:18.017 --> 00:21:21.670
the circulating libraries
brought than 23,000 books

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:22.503
to the provinces.

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:28.200
In 1942, just to zoom
in a little bit more,

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:32.009
to get a sense of the readers
and the reader tastes.

00:21:32.009 --> 00:21:35.564
In over 22 provinces as far as

00:21:35.564 --> 00:21:38.209
(listing Vietnamese provinces)

00:21:38.209 --> 00:21:39.830
participated in the
circulating library project

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:42.700
based out of the Saigon
Cochinchina library.

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:45.130
For example, the August
1942 borrowing lists

00:21:45.130 --> 00:21:47.660
from the port province of Rạch Giá

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:50.580
recorded 31 total borrowers for the month

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:52.689
of which two were French men,

00:21:52.689 --> 00:21:53.522
one was a Vietnamese woman,

00:21:53.522 --> 00:21:56.220
and the remaining 28
readers were Vietnamese men.

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:58.560
Most of the books borrowed
were French popular novels

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:02.510
such as pop fiction on romance,
police stories, espionage,

00:22:02.510 --> 00:22:05.750
by Delly, translations
of popular literature

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:08.540
like the work of Agatha
Christie, Pearl S. Buck

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.930
and Leo Tolstoy, as well as
French language historical works

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:15.710
on Napoleon and Caesar, books
on science and geography.

00:22:15.710 --> 00:22:18.853
In terms of Quốc Ngữ,
Vietnamese language works,

00:22:18.853 --> 00:22:20.110
the materials checked out
were instructional texts

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:23.240
as well as new popular Vietnamese novels.

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:26.160
As seen in this slide
these book wagon numbers

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:29.110
point to what Peter Zimmerman has termed

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:30.580
as a provincial cosmopolitanism

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:32.610
of popular, global and French literature

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:34.063
networked in Vietnam.

00:22:34.905 --> 00:22:36.240
And my research adds to this analysis

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:38.870
by highlighting the logistical
movement of these texts

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:40.620
into the countryside.

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:44.929
Furthermore, the book wagon's
circulation records show

00:22:44.929 --> 00:22:47.889
how the booming Vietnamese
Quốc Ngữ print market

00:22:47.889 --> 00:22:49.950
of new popular literature such as those by

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:52.320
Vietnamese Southern writer Hồ Biểu Chánh.

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:54.790
My research on the Bibliobus reveals

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:57.370
how these new experimental
forms of looking at literature

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:59.390
primarily produced in the urban centers

00:22:59.390 --> 00:23:02.820
of Hanoi and Saigon
extended geographically,

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:05.590
moving and conceptualizing
the Vietnamese reading public

00:23:05.590 --> 00:23:06.863
into the countryside.

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:10.520
Another major argument that I advance

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:12.150
is that the development of libraries

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:14.867
was not simply a French-initiated project

00:23:14.867 --> 00:23:17.650
but involved a larger community
of Vietnamese publishers,

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:20.060
journalists and literary figures

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:22.070
who called for the
development of libraries

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:25.080
for the larger Vietnamese
language reading public.

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:28.940
In 1942, Khuong Viet
drafted an extensive program

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:31.667
to create a Quốc Ngữ library system

00:23:31.667 --> 00:23:32.770
throughout the three regions of Vietnam

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:35.340
and published this four page
essay featured prominently

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:37.410
in the popular colonial
periodical "Tri Tan."

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:41.540
Khuong Viet was the penname
of Saigon-based journalist

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:42.920
and writer and Ngô Chân Lưu

00:23:44.108 --> 00:23:46.065
who had worked in the Saigon library

00:23:46.065 --> 00:23:48.240
in the 40's as well as
participated in associations

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:50.480
for the advancements of Quốc Ngữ.

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:52.750
In his essay, he directly
draws a connection

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:55.130
between the development
of Quốc Ngữ libraries

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:57.340
and the advancement of
Vietnamese civilization

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:58.663
and collective identity.

00:23:59.510 --> 00:24:01.920
The first part of the essay
establishes the pervasiveness

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:05.770
of Quốc Ngữ and the patriotic
responsibility of each person

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:08.417
in the country to serve
and improve the language,

00:24:08.417 --> 00:24:10.427
"since it directly
connects to the progress

00:24:10.427 --> 00:24:12.820
"of the motherland, " he quotes.

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:15.800
Taking a closer analysis of his essay,

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:19.264
as shown in this quote, Viet
points to the importance

00:24:19.264 --> 00:24:21.678
of spreading Quốc Ngữ works
to the larger collective

00:24:21.678 --> 00:24:23.470
and uses a tone emphasizing
the urgent necessity

00:24:23.470 --> 00:24:27.080
to think beyond the individual
and towards the collective.

00:24:27.080 --> 00:24:29.730
Viet framed the libraries
project as a collective duty.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:33.737
He says, "I have been able
to superfluously enjoy

00:24:33.737 --> 00:24:35.537
"these priceless spiritual nourishments.

00:24:35.537 --> 00:24:38.178
"I must not be selfish and
only enjoy these Quốc Ngữ works

00:24:38.178 --> 00:24:39.177
"for myself.

00:24:39.177 --> 00:24:40.327
"I must do something
so that my compatriots

00:24:40.327 --> 00:24:43.452
"less fortunate than I
who do not have money

00:24:43.452 --> 00:24:45.487
"to even witness and see these
valuable works of Quốc Ngữ

00:24:45.487 --> 00:24:48.950
"can benefit from these
books along with me."

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:51.280
At the foundation of this project,

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:54.530
Khuong Viet outlines a collective concept

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:56.400
of the Vietnamese public tied deeply

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:59.971
to the dutiful involvement
of the collective public

00:24:59.971 --> 00:25:01.259
or the Quan chung,

00:25:01.259 --> 00:25:02.960
for the mass general benefit,

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:05.578
regardless of class,
religion and politics.

00:25:05.578 --> 00:25:07.170
The author subtly critiques
existing libraries

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:09.976
in Indochina, stating that
private libraries of associations

00:25:09.976 --> 00:25:13.110
only permitted access to its members

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:14.980
while the state Hanoi Central Library

00:25:15.815 --> 00:25:17.100
made it difficult to enter and access

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:19.000
its Quốc Ngữ collection.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:20.830
Khuong Viet described his proposed project

00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:23.440
as a Quốc Ngữ new library
network with central libraries

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:25.440
first in Hanoi and Saigon,

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:28.560
bridging into provincial
libraries and then a book wagon,

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.770
bringing books to provinces and villages.

00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:33.430
Furthermore Viet argues
that the preservation

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:35.070
of Quốc Ngữ books are part of a project

00:25:35.070 --> 00:25:37.440
to preserve Vietnamese culture.

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:39.570
In analyzing this important essay,

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:40.680
I point to the relationship

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:42.580
between individual and collective duty

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:46.020
and how it intersects with the
consciousness of the public

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:48.690
and a sense of Vietnamese
collective evolution

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:50.730
as seen on the bottom of this slide.

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:53.368
Furthermore, Viet's
argument and word choice

00:25:53.368 --> 00:25:54.990
emphasizes how the public
work of building a library

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:55.920
could also contribute

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:58.560
to a broader cultural and national project

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:01.090
of defining and preserving a
sense of Vietnamese culture

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:02.563
through Quốc Ngữ collections.

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:06.100
Viet proposes circulating
library or book wagons initiative

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:09.023
which would bring Quốc
Ngữ books and newspapers

00:26:09.023 --> 00:26:11.016
directly to distant villages and hamlets.

00:26:11.016 --> 00:26:13.220
Viet described this as a
good popularization effort

00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:15.810
to bring the villager useful knowledge

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:17.720
for the betterment of their lives.

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:19.570
It's not surprising that
the proposed project

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:22.450
mirrors that of the
existing directorate system

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:25.290
and the proposals from Boudet,
given Viet's experience

00:26:26.549 --> 00:26:27.413
working in the Saigon library.

00:26:28.392 --> 00:26:30.440
Yet, so a lot of his
model, his proposed system

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:33.490
was modeled off of the
existing state Hanoi

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:34.390
and Saigon libraries.

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:36.580
Yet, the proposed system
distinguished itself

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:39.230
from the state libraries
due to its emphasis firstly

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:40.900
on Quốc Ngữ collections

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:43.640
and secondly on the
collective monetary investment

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:45.303
from the wider Vietnamese public.

00:26:46.911 --> 00:26:49.306
Furthermore Viet points
to the cultural capital

00:26:49.306 --> 00:26:51.490
of the library as an
appealing gathering place

00:26:51.490 --> 00:26:53.650
for the ultimate literary coterie

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:57.191
of Vietnamese educated
youth, writers, thinkers,

00:26:57.191 --> 00:26:58.080
and elite readers.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:00.330
This project is significant
because it points

00:27:00.330 --> 00:27:03.330
to how the creation of public
library was at its foundation

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:06.990
a collective endeavor tied to
Vietnamese national community

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:08.970
and responsibility of compatriots

00:27:09.829 --> 00:27:11.580
to cultivate, protect
and share that identity

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:12.793
according to Viet.

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:16.500
About a week after Viet's article,

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:19.459
the journalist and scholar
of literature and history

00:27:19.459 --> 00:27:22.810
Hoa Bang, expanded Khuong Viet's proposal

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:25.570
to include building reading societies

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:26.403
in the countryside.

00:27:26.403 --> 00:27:30.540
Also publish in "Tri Tan,"
the article (Vietnamese name)

00:27:34.232 --> 00:27:35.065
the city outskirts and the countryside

00:27:35.065 --> 00:27:37.390
need these reading societies explain

00:27:37.390 --> 00:27:40.673
that existing library
initiatives only serve

00:27:40.673 --> 00:27:43.770
a limited minority of urban
readers literate in French.

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:46.430
The author also criticizes
the Hanoi Central Library

00:27:46.430 --> 00:27:48.830
as restrictive, given
it's simplistic focus

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:51.930
on French material and
thus limiting its influence

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:54.490
to only a few and failing to provide

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:56.770
a universal service to all.

00:27:56.770 --> 00:27:58.640
Building upon Viet's linguistic demands

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:01.860
for more Quốc Ngữ libraries,
Bang also calls for

00:28:03.011 --> 00:28:04.874
development of these reading spaces

00:28:04.874 --> 00:28:07.990
beyond the urban confines
and into the rural outskirts

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:09.090
and countryside communities.

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:11.520
Specifically Hoa Bang argues

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:15.190
that cultivating reading
practices had a moralizing benefit

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:17.645
and thus should be universally applied

00:28:17.645 --> 00:28:18.800
across geographic reaches.

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:21.960
Firstly, you notice is how
the creation of libraries

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:23.150
supported good leisure

00:28:23.150 --> 00:28:25.728
and thus could offset
bad leisure behaviors.

00:28:25.728 --> 00:28:28.720
Besides the promotion of
healthy leisure activities,

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:31.640
Bang emphasized that every
day tastes must be cultivated

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:33.710
for the good of humanity.

00:28:33.710 --> 00:28:35.109
You can see this in the quote here

00:28:35.109 --> 00:28:36.160
where he discusses reading
books as an antidote

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:39.730
to bad leisure practices
such as going to taverns.

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:42.770
Building upon the metaphor
of reading as eating,

00:28:42.770 --> 00:28:45.810
Hoa Bang represents books
as desirable food objects

00:28:45.810 --> 00:28:48.867
with attractive senses,
spiritual nourishment

00:28:48.867 --> 00:28:50.240
in the second quote here in this slide.

00:28:50.240 --> 00:28:53.668
He notes how urban readers
already have a certain addiction

00:28:53.668 --> 00:28:55.170
to this type of literary consumption.

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:58.340
Hoa Bang proposed that reading
societies could be built

00:28:59.246 --> 00:29:00.669
in the existing temples of literature

00:29:00.669 --> 00:29:02.270
and shrines in the villages and hamlets.

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:05.040
He draws heavily from
Confucian values of scholarship

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:08.220
and examination to argue
that the inherent value

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:11.589
of literature and reading
honored through the creation

00:29:11.589 --> 00:29:12.700
of these reading societies.

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:16.050
Quoting a popular aphorism
among exam laureates

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:18.647
that translates as, "Even
if a child is stupid,

00:29:18.647 --> 00:29:21.170
"they must still try to
study and read literature."

00:29:21.170 --> 00:29:22.270
Hoa Bang declares

00:29:22.270 --> 00:29:25.060
how proposed reading
societies carry on the wishes

00:29:25.060 --> 00:29:27.750
of Vietnamese scholarly ancestors.

00:29:27.750 --> 00:29:30.710
Similar to Viet's proposal,
Bang concludes this essay

00:29:30.710 --> 00:29:33.290
with a statement that the
Quốc Ngữ Library project

00:29:33.290 --> 00:29:36.333
ultimate benefits, the public, the people.

00:29:36.333 --> 00:29:39.700
He calls for the individual
districts to sacrifice

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:42.440
50% of their budget used for festivals

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:45.803
to contribute to the developing
of these library projects.

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:50.150
The Bibliobus book wagons
project is significant

00:29:50.150 --> 00:29:52.530
because firstly it situates the library

00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:55.150
within the landscape of print culture

00:29:55.150 --> 00:29:57.747
and geography of colonial control.

00:29:57.747 --> 00:30:00.010
And secondly, it points to
the administrative exchanges

00:30:00.010 --> 00:30:01.410
between Imperial projects

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:03.630
and international library sciences.

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:06.600
I reveal the diffuse negotiations

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:09.460
and fragmented implementation
of an official system

00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:12.612
to control the publishing
and distribution of print

00:30:12.612 --> 00:30:15.330
through Indochina, motivated
by indirect propaganda.

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:18.615
These projects decentralized print control

00:30:18.615 --> 00:30:21.233
emerge out of colonial fears
of rising urban radicalism

00:30:21.233 --> 00:30:24.860
as well as anti-colonial
revolutionary ideologies.

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:27.613
In comparison Director of the Archives

00:30:27.613 --> 00:30:30.850
and Libraries, Paul Boudet,
focused on expanding access

00:30:30.850 --> 00:30:32.797
to quote "apolitical reading
matter for the countryside

00:30:32.797 --> 00:30:35.050
"through circulating book wagons."

00:30:35.050 --> 00:30:37.120
Boudet directly engaged with international

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:39.960
current and library practices
to bring good reading

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:42.880
directly to the people geographically,

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:45.620
drawing from the US, France, England,

00:30:45.620 --> 00:30:48.030
the movement saw the
creation of public libraries

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:50.270
as the actualization
of the state commitment

00:30:51.187 --> 00:30:53.370
to its citizens, access to
modern, valuable knowledge,

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:56.700
and expansion of public
services into the countryside.

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:58.340
Boudet presented his book wagons project

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:02.100
as one of vulgarization and
benevolent modernization.

00:31:02.100 --> 00:31:04.250
In this way the mission
and everyday reality

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:07.360
of colonial library carried
this inherently contradictory

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:08.670
ambiguous role.

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:11.440
The library reflected
state visions of control

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:14.087
modernian power, yet at the same time,

00:31:14.087 --> 00:31:16.688
the library advances benevolent mission

00:31:16.688 --> 00:31:17.521
of popular modern education,

00:31:17.521 --> 00:31:20.120
allowing for self-directed
selection of reading matter

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:23.100
and adapted to demands for public access.

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:25.590
But most importantly, my
close study of Bibliobus

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:28.840
sheds light on the mechanics
of books circulation

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:31.504
and the literary tastes
of Vietnamese readers

00:31:31.504 --> 00:31:32.337
in the provinces.

00:31:32.337 --> 00:31:34.300
I trace the mechanics of book circulation

00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:36.462
of book wagons project

00:31:36.462 --> 00:31:37.730
and illuminate how Vietnamese readers

00:31:38.886 --> 00:31:39.900
engage with this
provincial cosmopolitanism

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:41.470
of reading global literature,

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:43.340
as well as Vietnamese new literary forms

00:31:43.340 --> 00:31:45.970
in the urban centers of Hanoi and Saigon.

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:47.660
I uncover the vibrant debates

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:50.490
amongst Vietnamese intellectuals
on the pages of "Tri Tan"

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:54.008
who called out the failures
of colonial state projects

00:31:54.008 --> 00:31:56.208
to provide adequate and
equal library access

00:31:56.208 --> 00:31:59.644
and Quốc Ngữ reading materials
to the Vietnamese public

00:31:59.644 --> 00:32:01.270
especially those in the countryside.

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:03.920
These intellectuals
proposed a distinct meaning

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:07.020
of the public, rooted in
a collective consciousness

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:10.821
through the selfless act of
building literary collections,

00:32:10.821 --> 00:32:11.910
specifically in Quốc Ngữ.

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:15.130
This tied national identity
to linguistic identity

00:32:15.130 --> 00:32:18.520
and Quốc Ngữ as a national
language of the masses.

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:21.030
Emphasizing the inherent
value of Quốc Ngữ texts,

00:32:21.030 --> 00:32:24.885
Khuong Viet and Hoa Bang
define Vietnamese good reading

00:32:24.885 --> 00:32:27.310
as a collective project
that involved all Vietnamese

00:32:27.310 --> 00:32:29.010
including those in the countryside,

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:31.910
beyond the urban centers
of Hanoi and Saigon.

00:32:31.910 --> 00:32:33.550
Good reading was not just texts,

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:36.260
but was a way of defining,
modernizing and uplifting

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:39.270
Vietnamese leisure practices,
spiritual influences

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:41.190
and intellectual nourishment.

00:32:41.190 --> 00:32:43.230
Furthermore, good reading
and the preservation

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:45.220
of Quốc Ngữ libraries contributed

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:48.170
to defining a Vietnamese
collective cultural identity

00:32:48.170 --> 00:32:49.910
and historical temporality

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:53.010
from pre-colonial Confucian
scholarly traditions

00:32:53.010 --> 00:32:56.290
into the present and into the future.

00:32:56.290 --> 00:32:58.560
So this excerpt formed a key component

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:01.706
of my fourth chapter on the
politics of good reading

00:33:01.706 --> 00:33:02.539
and print control.

00:33:02.539 --> 00:33:04.680
In the brief remaining time,

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:07.560
I'm just going to breeze through
and zoom out a little bit

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:10.181
on how this fits into the
larger manuscript project.

00:33:10.181 --> 00:33:13.740
So in this reading, as a book project,

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:17.020
the chapters follow a chronological
and thematic structure.

00:33:17.020 --> 00:33:18.560
The first focusing on builders,

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:22.006
then the readers, popular
libraries, print control,

00:33:22.006 --> 00:33:22.839
the excerpt I read today,

00:33:22.839 --> 00:33:25.370
and then concluding with decolonization.

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:27.240
Each chapter considers the relationship

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:29.130
between print control and the public

00:33:29.130 --> 00:33:31.400
as well as thinking about
the historical development

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:34.030
of libraries as spaces
of reading, education

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:35.917
and documentation.

00:33:35.917 --> 00:33:38.210
Given this limited time,
I'll just briefly discuss

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:41.080
the scope of the first two
chapters and last chapter.

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:43.840
So for example, in chapter one,

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:46.320
I establish the histories,
vision and key personnel

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:48.400
of the four central institutions
examined in the book:

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:50.665
the Saigon Cochinchina library,

00:33:50.665 --> 00:33:53.240
the Hanoi Central Library,
Phnom Penh library

00:33:53.240 --> 00:33:56.962
in Cambodia, and the Directorate
of Archives and Libraries

00:33:56.962 --> 00:33:57.962
in Indochina.

00:33:57.962 --> 00:33:59.000
I argue that French colonialism gave rise

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.400
to new types of hybrid
library institutions

00:34:01.400 --> 00:34:04.220
shaped by the changing
politics of the colonial state

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:06.710
and library standards of print control.

00:34:06.710 --> 00:34:08.100
I pay particular attention

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:11.580
to both high level
labor, trained in France

00:34:11.580 --> 00:34:13.780
in library sciences
and to the tech economy

00:34:13.780 --> 00:34:18.400
such as the Chartres
Administrators in this slide.

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:20.130
But I also pay particular attention

00:34:20.130 --> 00:34:22.700
to the training of Vietnamese laborers

00:34:22.700 --> 00:34:24.660
as seen in this slide.

00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:27.320
As a counter narrative to the
first chapter on builders,

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:30.770
this chapter focuses on
library users and experiences

00:34:30.770 --> 00:34:32.170
in the Central Hanoi Library

00:34:33.059 --> 00:34:35.097
and Cochinchina Library in Saigon.

00:34:35.097 --> 00:34:37.720
I focus on the symbolic
and experience architecture

00:34:37.720 --> 00:34:38.750
of the library.

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:40.630
This chapter sheds light on the ways

00:34:41.554 --> 00:34:44.040
that the library reader
bodies were made modern.

00:34:44.040 --> 00:34:46.960
They're legible, quieted,
restricted and monitored.

00:34:46.960 --> 00:34:48.700
Specifically I examined race, space,

00:34:48.700 --> 00:34:51.180
restrictions to library
access, and regulations

00:34:51.180 --> 00:34:55.830
on proper behavior to
demonstrate the contradiction

00:34:55.830 --> 00:34:58.218
of colonial Republican
project to modernize

00:34:58.218 --> 00:35:01.190
through exclusion and racial hierarchy.

00:35:01.190 --> 00:35:03.321
Yet by the 20s and 30s,

00:35:03.321 --> 00:35:06.380
Vietnamese students became
the majority of library users

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:09.602
and subverted this colonial
vision of the library

00:35:09.602 --> 00:35:12.220
as an exclusive space for French readers,

00:35:12.220 --> 00:35:14.220
colonial administrators
and scholarly researchers.

00:35:14.220 --> 00:35:16.220
I argue that the Vietnamese library users

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:18.920
redefined the meaning
of modern public space

00:35:18.920 --> 00:35:21.502
by contesting library reading
roles, demanding reform

00:35:21.502 --> 00:35:24.540
and increased access and
re-inscribing the actual

00:35:24.540 --> 00:35:27.840
and symbolic space of the library
through everyday behavior.

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:30.270
I continue on through the chapters

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:31.840
to conclude with decolonization

00:35:31.840 --> 00:35:34.190
where I examine the decolonization

00:35:34.190 --> 00:35:36.490
of this elaborate Directorate
of Archives and Libraries

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:37.323
in Indochina

00:35:38.220 --> 00:35:39.770
as well as the nationalization
of central libraries

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:42.736
in the democratic Republic
of Vietnam in Hanoi

00:35:42.736 --> 00:35:44.240
and the Republic of Vietnam in Saigon.

00:35:44.240 --> 00:35:47.330
I reveal how decolonization
was this fragmented process

00:35:47.330 --> 00:35:50.340
embedded within the
logistical movement of people,

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:53.310
reading matter and
physically creating spaces

00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:56.960
as well as the redefinition
of colonial institutions

00:35:56.960 --> 00:35:59.260
in a post-colonial national landscape.

00:35:59.260 --> 00:36:00.840
So here you have a photograph

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:04.167
of President Ngo Dinh Diem
inaugurating the building

00:36:04.167 --> 00:36:05.500
of this new ambitious
national library project

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:09.050
and cultural center in Saigon in 1956.

00:36:09.050 --> 00:36:10.890
And this was actually
built on the former site

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:12.093
of the colonial prison.

00:36:14.084 --> 00:36:16.160
So just to summarize,
as shown in this visual

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:18.510
through the book, I
uncover how the library

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:21.700
is at the center of two
intertwined processes:

00:36:21.700 --> 00:36:24.370
the development of the
colonial state and the public.

00:36:24.370 --> 00:36:25.910
This dynamic history of the library

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:28.421
in colonial and post-colonial Vietnam

00:36:28.421 --> 00:36:30.180
reveals a hybrid model of state libraries,

00:36:30.180 --> 00:36:31.810
which are constantly negotiating

00:36:31.810 --> 00:36:34.250
between different information systems,

00:36:34.250 --> 00:36:37.510
different visions of reading
public, access and education,

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:40.700
as well as the role of
modern state institutions.

00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:44.600
So taking a larger step back
and transitioning into Q&amp;A,

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:46.430
I just wanted to share
what's been on my mind

00:36:46.430 --> 00:36:48.950
as I worked through the
book manuscript revisions

00:36:48.950 --> 00:36:51.884
and thinking of the library
as this analytical lens.

00:36:51.884 --> 00:36:54.730
So I'm working on how
the library allows us

00:36:54.730 --> 00:36:56.750
to understand larger cultural, social

00:36:56.750 --> 00:36:59.077
and literary processes.

00:36:59.077 --> 00:37:01.280
So firstly, I situate the library

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:02.820
within reading and print culture

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:05.010
and reveal how libraries
are important sites

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:07.600
of cosmopolitan and textual exchange,

00:37:07.600 --> 00:37:10.410
as well as understanding the
different historic processes

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:12.060
of language learning and development

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:13.900
of French and Vietnamese.

00:37:13.900 --> 00:37:17.010
And in ways that are part of
the landscape of other spaces

00:37:17.010 --> 00:37:19.225
that are occurring during this time,

00:37:19.225 --> 00:37:21.990
bookshops, associations,
clubs, temples and schools,

00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:24.485
but also libraries are distinct

00:37:24.485 --> 00:37:27.820
because of the nature
of how individuals read

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:32.010
through self-selection and
self-directed reading matter,

00:37:32.010 --> 00:37:36.280
as well as public reading
practices in institutional spaces

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:39.730
such as in Hanoi and Saigon libraries.

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:41.730
For the second point, I'm investigating

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:43.460
the role of libraries as an institution

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:46.150
of state documentation of its subjects

00:37:46.150 --> 00:37:49.743
and evolving bureaucratic
technical practices.

00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:52.440
Here I seek to contribute new insight

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:55.670
into the field of library
science and information history

00:37:55.670 --> 00:37:58.280
where I approach the
concept of the library

00:37:58.280 --> 00:38:00.510
as both a material repository

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:04.670
and a set of technical,
cultural, and social practices.

00:38:04.670 --> 00:38:07.990
And lastly, how I engage
with my archival newspaper

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:09.780
and literary source material

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:11.800
is to provide a cultural history

00:38:11.800 --> 00:38:15.470
of urban public spaces and
practices of public reading.

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:17.370
Specifically that means investigating

00:38:17.370 --> 00:38:19.450
and paying attention to the ways

00:38:19.450 --> 00:38:21.830
in which these historical
actors are occupying

00:38:21.830 --> 00:38:24.660
or reclaiming space in ways
that are often not intended

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:25.793
by administrators.

00:38:26.726 --> 00:38:29.760
Thinking about these spaces
as social or leisure spaces,

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:34.087
idle spaces, how individuals
seem to constantly be breaking,

00:38:34.087 --> 00:38:37.380
breaking rules in the library, providing,

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:41.730
being noisy, being disorderly,
disrupting other readers,

00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:43.620
but also the ways in which the spaces

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:45.433
are gendered and racialized.

00:38:46.370 --> 00:38:49.580
So, I mean, that's just
by way of conclusion

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:52.400
which are the concepts that
are evolving in my mind.

00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:54.500
I very much look forward to Q&amp;A

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:58.140
as well as just any other
questions about my work

00:38:58.140 --> 00:39:00.000
and how it intersects with yours

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:03.290
or as well as just meeting
all of the attendees here.

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:04.193
Thank you so much.

00:39:10.026 --> 00:39:11.560
- Thank you, Cindy Nguyen.

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:16.110
I will help moderate the Q&amp;A session.

00:39:16.110 --> 00:39:19.210
So if you could please
use the raised hand.

00:39:19.210 --> 00:39:21.950
There's already a couple
of questions in the chat,

00:39:21.950 --> 00:39:24.919
and then we'll queue up the questions.

00:39:24.919 --> 00:39:28.010
And we ask that, you
know, if you could please

00:39:28.010 --> 00:39:31.930
go on camera and and direct
your question to Dr. Nguyen.

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:36.930
So first up we have Professor Hue-Tam Tai.

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:41.360
who's dropped two questions
in here for Cindy.

00:39:41.360 --> 00:39:45.403
So could you please raise your
question, Professor Hue Tai?

00:39:47.900 --> 00:39:51.600
- I have quite a few questions

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:56.263
because your project is
really interesting to me.

00:39:57.950 --> 00:40:02.470
The first one was, was
the Bibliobus a project

00:40:02.470 --> 00:40:05.230
meant to counter specifically

00:40:05.230 --> 00:40:09.030
the influence of Vietnamese journalism

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:12.050
that originated in the cities

00:40:12.050 --> 00:40:14.523
but was spread through the provinces,

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:20.600
especially after the wave
of protest of 1926, 1927?

00:40:23.690 --> 00:40:28.690
So since the Bibliobus project
seems to have been launched

00:40:30.380 --> 00:40:35.380
in 1928, was there, you know, the idea

00:40:35.480 --> 00:40:40.470
of countering these
protests and the influence

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:45.427
of young Franco-Annamite
trained protesters

00:40:47.212 --> 00:40:49.133
in the countryside?

00:40:50.870 --> 00:40:53.537
So that's my first question.

00:40:53.537 --> 00:40:58.537
And I hope that your work
does address that question.

00:41:00.530 --> 00:41:02.310
- Well, firstly, thank you so much

00:41:02.310 --> 00:41:06.390
for raising that question,
and your book on radicalism

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:10.550
definitely has informed and
shapes a lot of my work.

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:14.830
So the short answer is partially yes.

00:41:14.830 --> 00:41:19.537
A large significant part
of the the description

00:41:19.537 --> 00:41:20.370
specifically from Vayrac

00:41:20.370 --> 00:41:24.990
about urban intellectuals
and even like the coining

00:41:26.330 --> 00:41:29.430
or the framing of radicalism
and Bolshevik ideologies

00:41:29.430 --> 00:41:33.080
was very much a discussion
about the specific response

00:41:33.080 --> 00:41:36.490
to the influence of Vietnamese journalism

00:41:36.490 --> 00:41:38.763
and the protests in 26, 27.

00:41:39.980 --> 00:41:41.070
So I say partially yes

00:41:41.070 --> 00:41:45.390
because the other aspect of this project

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:49.010
was that it was a continual
large scale project

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:50.940
involving many actors, of course,

00:41:50.940 --> 00:41:52.773
Pierre Pasquier and Vayrac,

00:41:53.758 --> 00:41:56.080
but then also several other,

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:58.680
several other colonial administrators.

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:03.680
And then the focus on looking
at Indonesia as an example,

00:42:08.860 --> 00:42:10.750
or the Dutch East Indies as an example

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:15.750
of how to elaborate and create
this system to counter this

00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:19.203
was also kind of intersected and,

00:42:20.120 --> 00:42:21.107
intersected with those protests

00:42:21.107 --> 00:42:24.437
but also pre-dated those protests as well

00:42:24.437 --> 00:42:25.340
because there were initiatives

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:29.850
looking at indigenous
publishing since the early 20's.

00:42:29.850 --> 00:42:33.970
The specifics of Vayrac as
he talks about the urban

00:42:35.455 --> 00:42:38.480
versus rural tension and sense of threat

00:42:38.480 --> 00:42:40.410
was definitely a response to the protest.

00:42:40.410 --> 00:42:42.890
But I just wanted to situate
this in a longer history

00:42:42.890 --> 00:42:44.840
of thinking about indigenous publishing

00:42:45.799 --> 00:42:49.670
and forms of subsidizing and
promoting different types

00:42:49.670 --> 00:42:52.310
of quote, politically
safe texts and publishing.

00:42:52.310 --> 00:42:53.960
And then the second part of this,

00:42:55.070 --> 00:42:58.590
of the ways in which the project pre-dated

00:42:58.590 --> 00:43:03.590
and expanded beyond the specifics
of these 26, 27 protests

00:43:04.575 --> 00:43:06.630
is in the role of Boudet
who actually had proposed

00:43:06.630 --> 00:43:11.400
the Bibliobus, the book
wagons project since 1917

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:13.490
and then later since 1919.

00:43:13.490 --> 00:43:18.030
So he envisioned forms of
at least distributing books

00:43:18.030 --> 00:43:21.070
and the mechanism of how
one could distribute books

00:43:21.070 --> 00:43:24.100
by drawing from a more
global library approach

00:43:24.100 --> 00:43:25.980
of looking at public libraries

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:27.890
and book wagons that was stemming from

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:30.410
the American Library Association

00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:33.610
and stuff like rural libraries
projects that were developed

00:43:33.610 --> 00:43:37.210
in America, actually since
the turn of the 19th century.

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:41.200
So then there's different
initiatives that are happening

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:42.090
at the same time.

00:43:42.090 --> 00:43:43.850
And I think that's why this chapter was,

00:43:43.850 --> 00:43:46.320
or this excerpt was so
hard to parse through

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:50.100
is that there wasn't one
singular justification,

00:43:50.100 --> 00:43:51.710
and the justification is varied

00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:54.260
because Boudat when talking
to Pasquier, of course,

00:43:55.335 --> 00:43:56.854
like framed his book wagons project

00:43:56.854 --> 00:43:59.570
as one of counter prop, like
ways of countering radicalism

00:43:59.570 --> 00:44:01.340
but actually in practice often

00:44:01.340 --> 00:44:03.970
he was just discussing the ways in which

00:44:03.970 --> 00:44:06.110
how does one carry out this

00:44:06.110 --> 00:44:08.240
in the context of public libraries

00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:10.673
and more of a libraries apparatus.

00:44:14.237 --> 00:44:17.710
- Okay, anybody else wants
to raise the question

00:44:17.710 --> 00:44:20.543
'cause I've got quite a few more.

00:44:22.280 --> 00:44:24.980
Since the Bibliobus was

00:44:28.240 --> 00:44:32.660
catering to both French
and Vietnamese readers,

00:44:35.620 --> 00:44:38.560
what was the difference in the materials

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:40.063
that were being circulated?

00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:43.670
For French, for example,

00:44:43.670 --> 00:44:47.013
were they mostly for
entertainment purposes,

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:49.723
you know, reading for leisure?

00:44:51.092 --> 00:44:55.460
For Vietnamese, was there a
more educational propaganda

00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:57.140
element to it?

00:44:57.140 --> 00:45:01.180
I mean, how would we
know how to differentiate

00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:05.140
and what was the decision-making process

00:45:05.140 --> 00:45:10.140
for, you know, choosing what
book to carry to the provinces?

00:45:12.470 --> 00:45:14.870
- Thank you so much for that question.

00:45:14.870 --> 00:45:17.730
It's a really hard question to answer

00:45:17.730 --> 00:45:20.500
given the archival records,
but how I understand it

00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:23.390
was that often,

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:27.760
so the reason why I also
discussed the space limitations

00:45:27.760 --> 00:45:30.351
in the Cochinchina library reading room

00:45:30.351 --> 00:45:31.930
was that there were only 44 seats.

00:45:31.930 --> 00:45:35.960
And in actuality, the reading
room was constantly full.

00:45:35.960 --> 00:45:39.192
There were a lot of readers
actually got turned away.

00:45:39.192 --> 00:45:41.032
So this is urban readers were turned away,

00:45:41.032 --> 00:45:44.010
and the lending section
was incredibly popular

00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:45.570
because of the limited space for readers

00:45:45.570 --> 00:45:46.870
to actually read in place.

00:45:47.812 --> 00:45:50.920
So often readers then borrowed
books to read somewhere else

00:45:51.824 --> 00:45:53.400
not in the actual reading room.

00:45:53.400 --> 00:45:56.030
So my understanding is
that the lending section

00:45:56.030 --> 00:46:01.030
is what then continues to be
circulated into the provinces.

00:46:01.450 --> 00:46:04.133
So these lending section collections,

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:07.600
the goal of the library
was that they hoped

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:11.500
that they would reflect
larger kind of demands

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:12.920
and reader tastes.

00:46:12.920 --> 00:46:14.580
So I think this is one of the challenges,

00:46:14.580 --> 00:46:17.320
and I think those who are in libraries,

00:46:17.320 --> 00:46:19.820
work in libraries could
speak to this as well

00:46:19.820 --> 00:46:24.820
is that libraries have
two interwoven tensions.

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:28.290
And I think you also quote references too

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:31.030
which is the initiative
to preserve or to conserve

00:46:31.030 --> 00:46:32.420
and to circulate.

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:36.290
And at times those
could work hand in hand,

00:46:36.290 --> 00:46:37.833
but then if you are,

00:46:39.220 --> 00:46:40.920
essentially the more you circulate a work,

00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:44.180
the more they actually,
the books get destroyed.

00:46:44.180 --> 00:46:47.040
And the more that you
have to put more attention

00:46:47.917 --> 00:46:49.240
into actual like preservation.

00:46:49.240 --> 00:46:51.043
So they are like two interwoven missions.

00:46:52.196 --> 00:46:53.830
And then the, and then those, that mission

00:46:53.830 --> 00:46:57.230
basically underlies the
quote unquote success

00:46:58.797 --> 00:47:00.770
of a library is that the
library is extensively used

00:47:00.770 --> 00:47:02.418
really well.

00:47:02.418 --> 00:47:04.020
It's circulated really
well, but it also preserves

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:05.599
and builds a large collection

00:47:05.599 --> 00:47:08.780
that reflects certain
collecting practices.

00:47:08.780 --> 00:47:11.290
And the collecting
practices and philosophy

00:47:12.199 --> 00:47:16.770
of what to collect was in
principle very idealistic,

00:47:16.770 --> 00:47:19.300
reflecting this sense
of, like I was saying

00:47:20.377 --> 00:47:21.710
with Paul Boudet is something that's like

00:47:23.018 --> 00:47:24.494
has collected all levels of knowledge,

00:47:24.494 --> 00:47:27.695
as extremely comprehensive,
reflects primarily

00:47:27.695 --> 00:47:31.840
the understanding of what
is considered valuable

00:47:31.840 --> 00:47:35.640
which is modeled upon
French notions of value.

00:47:35.640 --> 00:47:37.150
So that's a lot of classics.

00:47:37.150 --> 00:47:39.810
A lot of works, encyclopedias
were collected as well.

00:47:39.810 --> 00:47:40.763
A lot of volumes.

00:47:41.659 --> 00:47:43.290
There was an attempt to
build large collections

00:47:43.290 --> 00:47:45.750
of what was considered like the essential,

00:47:45.750 --> 00:47:47.570
the essential works.

00:47:47.570 --> 00:47:50.610
At the same time, there
were also other reflections

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:51.710
of what was interesting

00:47:51.710 --> 00:47:56.050
which is contemporary,
more contemporary materials

00:47:56.050 --> 00:47:58.100
that are published in Indochina,

00:47:58.100 --> 00:48:00.580
in the actual print sphere.

00:48:00.580 --> 00:48:04.023
So that could be bulletins that
could be other periodicals.

00:48:04.980 --> 00:48:07.370
And then the lending section
to answer your question

00:48:07.370 --> 00:48:11.453
was then built off of
more recent publications,

00:48:12.330 --> 00:48:15.900
and often the collections policy

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:18.660
and what circulated was often random

00:48:20.019 --> 00:48:22.900
based on donations,
based on cheap editions

00:48:22.900 --> 00:48:24.210
of what is popular.

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:27.304
And thus that is, that becomes cheaper.

00:48:27.304 --> 00:48:31.870
So for example, you have many editions

00:48:31.870 --> 00:48:34.380
of what you see as global best-sellers

00:48:34.380 --> 00:48:36.797
such as "The Count of Monte Cristo,"

00:48:36.797 --> 00:48:40.463
"The Three Musketeers,"
seen as kind of pop,

00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:47.221
not high class, high level literature,

00:48:47.221 --> 00:48:48.490
but readily available.

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:51.890
And those end up being
circulated much more readily

00:48:51.890 --> 00:48:54.660
and in the Bibliobus
because they're cheaper

00:48:54.660 --> 00:48:56.610
and then there's many editions of them.

00:49:00.642 --> 00:49:01.475
- All right, thanks Cindy.

00:49:01.475 --> 00:49:04.630
Next we have a couple hands raised here,

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:07.660
and Paul Sarno was the first person

00:49:07.660 --> 00:49:09.738
that raised his hand in here.

00:49:09.738 --> 00:49:11.341
So please go ahead.

00:49:11.341 --> 00:49:12.500
- Yes, I wanted to ask you.

00:49:12.500 --> 00:49:17.140
I saw those beautiful colonial buildings

00:49:17.140 --> 00:49:20.303
that constituted libraries,

00:49:20.303 --> 00:49:22.263
especially the one I saw in Cambodia.

00:49:23.378 --> 00:49:25.910
I think it had a date 1924 or so.

00:49:25.910 --> 00:49:28.450
What's happened to those buildings?

00:49:28.450 --> 00:49:30.810
And what's happened to the contents?

00:49:30.810 --> 00:49:34.640
And if you could start with
the Cambodian structure,

00:49:34.640 --> 00:49:35.763
I'd be interested.

00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:38.320
- Thank you so much, Paul.

00:49:38.320 --> 00:49:42.310
And this is where the Cambodia case is one

00:49:43.693 --> 00:49:45.467
that I feel very regretful about mainly

00:49:48.570 --> 00:49:50.460
because I was supposed
to do field research

00:49:50.460 --> 00:49:53.640
in the Cambodian library this past summer,

00:49:53.640 --> 00:49:56.870
but then that didn't end up happening

00:49:56.870 --> 00:49:58.303
amongst many other things.

00:49:59.230 --> 00:50:03.765
But from what I understand
is that that actually

00:50:03.765 --> 00:50:05.480
that building is now the archives,

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:07.180
the National Archives of Cambodia.

00:50:08.270 --> 00:50:10.710
And that is, in that sense,

00:50:10.710 --> 00:50:14.070
it retained its administrative apparatus.

00:50:14.070 --> 00:50:16.550
But then, I mean, this is,

00:50:16.550 --> 00:50:18.650
there's a lot of this is
like through conversations

00:50:18.650 --> 00:50:22.063
with my mentor Penny Edwards

00:50:22.063 --> 00:50:23.444
and the importance of actually,

00:50:23.444 --> 00:50:25.159
the need to do this type of work.

00:50:25.159 --> 00:50:27.244
Because like during the Khmer Rouge,

00:50:27.244 --> 00:50:32.088
like this area, this building
was, of course not used,

00:50:32.088 --> 00:50:35.548
but then like had other, was
occupied for different reasons

00:50:35.548 --> 00:50:38.520
from and then some of the
materials were either destroyed

00:50:38.520 --> 00:50:42.850
or, and at times actually
destruction of library materials

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:45.605
happens due to neglect,

00:50:45.605 --> 00:50:47.690
not necessarily out of intention.

00:50:47.690 --> 00:50:51.070
So a lot of the materials,
as many of you know,

00:50:51.070 --> 00:50:53.410
who have done work in Southeast Asia

00:50:54.344 --> 00:50:58.164
because of the humidity,
because of all like the bugs

00:50:58.164 --> 00:51:01.287
and the insects, then get
degraded quite quickly

00:51:01.287 --> 00:51:02.843
especially because of the papers.

00:51:02.843 --> 00:51:04.883
So a lot of the materials
are not in great condition.

00:51:06.320 --> 00:51:09.010
The Cambodian case too is interesting

00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:13.220
because the attempt to
build this elaborate

00:51:13.220 --> 00:51:15.890
Directorate of Archives and Library system

00:51:15.890 --> 00:51:18.210
in the five areas of Indochina

00:51:19.402 --> 00:51:22.460
really was this aspirational aspect

00:51:22.460 --> 00:51:25.726
where, you know, the Hanoi
Central Library would be

00:51:25.726 --> 00:51:27.746
almost like the equivalent
of a national library,

00:51:27.746 --> 00:51:30.890
and it had these subsequent
other headquarters

00:51:30.890 --> 00:51:33.433
based on the different countries.

00:51:34.420 --> 00:51:36.923
So you have Tonkin, you have Annam,

00:51:37.996 --> 00:51:40.846
you have Cochinchina in the
South, and then you have Laos

00:51:41.856 --> 00:51:44.634
and you have Cambodge, and
they were supposed to have

00:51:44.634 --> 00:51:46.590
like their own specific headquarters.

00:51:46.590 --> 00:51:47.460
So the Cambodge case is one.

00:51:47.460 --> 00:51:49.954
The Cochinchina Library
library is another,

00:51:49.954 --> 00:51:51.475
and then North central library.

00:51:51.475 --> 00:51:53.975
There were attempts to
build one north and in Laos

00:51:54.810 --> 00:51:56.790
but then those didn't really pick up.

00:51:56.790 --> 00:51:57.850
However, the Cambodia case is interesting

00:51:57.850 --> 00:52:01.230
because there was two
parallel major libraries

00:52:01.230 --> 00:52:02.063
that developed.

00:52:02.955 --> 00:52:06.770
The picture, that beautiful
library that you noted,

00:52:06.770 --> 00:52:11.050
that actually uses more
of a neoclassical style

00:52:11.050 --> 00:52:12.373
in terms of the facade.

00:52:13.440 --> 00:52:15.410
This library that was supposed to reflect

00:52:15.410 --> 00:52:18.793
this administrative colonial
library in Phnom Penh.

00:52:19.730 --> 00:52:22.992
But at the same time, Suzanne
Karpelès was working closely

00:52:22.992 --> 00:52:25.600
with the Buddhist Institute
and the Royal Library

00:52:25.600 --> 00:52:28.340
which had, which kind of predated

00:52:28.340 --> 00:52:30.720
the administrative library.

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:32.930
So there were two libraries
that were existing.

00:52:32.930 --> 00:52:35.580
One that the administrative library

00:52:35.580 --> 00:52:37.430
which was primarily
serving administrators,

00:52:37.430 --> 00:52:41.354
but kind of lost its sense
of purpose in a sense.

00:52:41.354 --> 00:52:42.704
Like it didn't have a full,

00:52:44.253 --> 00:52:46.358
a public facing library function.

00:52:46.358 --> 00:52:48.296
It was more of a
reference, administrative,

00:52:48.296 --> 00:52:50.290
almost seemed more like an
archive for administrators.

00:52:50.290 --> 00:52:54.920
And then the Royal Library then became,

00:52:54.920 --> 00:52:58.072
the Royal Library and
Buddhist Institute was more

00:52:58.072 --> 00:53:03.072
as part of the vision to
popularize print and Khmer script

00:53:03.890 --> 00:53:06.740
and part of nationalizing Buddhism,

00:53:06.740 --> 00:53:08.997
like that, that was the initiative

00:53:08.997 --> 00:53:10.600
that like Suzanne Karpelès
was deeply involved in.

00:53:10.600 --> 00:53:13.020
And that kind of carried
out through the lens

00:53:13.873 --> 00:53:18.190
more of cultural heritage, as
well as religious preservation

00:53:18.190 --> 00:53:23.190
and ways of building a vernacular
print sphere in Cambodia.

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:25.990
So then there's like two library systems

00:53:25.990 --> 00:53:27.500
that are very different in Cambodia.

00:53:27.500 --> 00:53:29.530
And I hope to continue to do my work.

00:53:29.530 --> 00:53:32.260
And expand into projects in Cambodia.

00:53:32.260 --> 00:53:36.080
I just, at this moment, that's
kind of my understanding

00:53:36.080 --> 00:53:37.333
of the institutional history.

00:53:40.219 --> 00:53:41.217
- ThanK you.

00:53:41.217 --> 00:53:42.407
- All right.

00:53:42.407 --> 00:53:43.340
So next up, we have Eric Harns,

00:53:43.340 --> 00:53:45.950
and then Al Lim and Michelle Thompson

00:53:47.443 --> 00:53:49.933
and also Dr. Noltan Yan.

00:53:51.080 --> 00:53:54.173
Cindy Nguyen has agreed
to go a little bit longer

00:53:54.173 --> 00:53:57.283
than the 1:00 p.m., so we'll
keep those questions going.

00:53:58.510 --> 00:54:00.240
So Eric, go ahead.

00:54:00.240 --> 00:54:01.073
- Thanks, Cindy.

00:54:01.073 --> 00:54:02.720
This is just so fascinating,

00:54:02.720 --> 00:54:05.173
such interesting work that you're doing,

00:54:05.173 --> 00:54:07.410
and I just wanna congratulate
you on the project overall

00:54:07.410 --> 00:54:10.053
and for a nice presentation today too.

00:54:12.371 --> 00:54:14.513
When you mentioned about Paul Boudet

00:54:14.513 --> 00:54:17.380
and this idea of wanting to compete

00:54:17.380 --> 00:54:21.306
with the illicit reading
material, you know,

00:54:21.306 --> 00:54:23.987
like the Bibliobus and the
library system in general

00:54:23.987 --> 00:54:25.120
it seems, right?

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:29.320
It made me want to know more
about the illicit materials,

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:32.874
and Hue-Tam already asked the question

00:54:32.874 --> 00:54:34.471
I was thinking of at first, you know,

00:54:34.471 --> 00:54:35.510
like about the journalism,

00:54:35.510 --> 00:54:38.390
but it also did open up another question

00:54:38.390 --> 00:54:40.891
I was thinking about like, well,

00:54:40.891 --> 00:54:43.528
what are all the things
that people are reading

00:54:43.528 --> 00:54:45.620
that are illicit and where
are they getting them?

00:54:45.620 --> 00:54:47.273
And how's it circulating?

00:54:48.590 --> 00:54:50.710
Because I remember when
reading about journalism,

00:54:50.710 --> 00:54:53.530
one of the things I think
in Hue-Tam's book actually,

00:54:53.530 --> 00:54:56.600
you know, Nguyễn An Ninh
publishes "La Cloche Fêlée,"

00:54:56.600 --> 00:55:00.060
and it has a certain, you
know, number of printed copies,

00:55:00.060 --> 00:55:02.410
but of course people are getting one copy

00:55:02.410 --> 00:55:05.812
and like 40 people are reading
it or something like that,

00:55:05.812 --> 00:55:07.430
you know, a whole bunch of people are.

00:55:07.430 --> 00:55:10.460
And so the, in a sense, it's
kind of like a local library,

00:55:10.460 --> 00:55:15.273
like they're, like a kind of
Bibliobus of the people, right?

00:55:16.280 --> 00:55:19.175
And so I'm just wondering,
like, what are people buying?

00:55:19.175 --> 00:55:20.173
Where are they going?

00:55:20.173 --> 00:55:21.210
What are the bookstores like in all this?

00:55:21.210 --> 00:55:23.370
Is there also regulation of what's sold

00:55:23.370 --> 00:55:25.070
in the bookstores and all of that?

00:55:25.990 --> 00:55:26.823
And so a general description

00:55:26.823 --> 00:55:29.930
of the reading public and
other forms of circulation

00:55:29.930 --> 00:55:31.993
would be really great to hear briefly.

00:55:34.193 --> 00:55:35.191
- Thank you, Eric.

00:55:35.191 --> 00:55:36.083
And I think this is where,

00:55:37.211 --> 00:55:38.140
this is very much deeply on my mind

00:55:38.140 --> 00:55:40.130
is how do I place the libraries

00:55:40.130 --> 00:55:42.740
within this larger
ecosystem of print sphere?

00:55:42.740 --> 00:55:45.860
Because of course,
individuals are reading widely

00:55:45.860 --> 00:55:48.430
from all these different sources from,

00:55:48.430 --> 00:55:50.030
as you noted, like bookstores.

00:55:50.030 --> 00:55:52.014
They're passing on the materials.

00:55:52.014 --> 00:55:54.560
They're participating in new forms

00:55:54.560 --> 00:55:57.250
of like serialized storytelling

00:55:57.250 --> 00:55:59.310
as well as different types
of journalistic practices.

00:55:59.310 --> 00:56:01.760
And I would also like
defer this type of work to

00:56:01.760 --> 00:56:06.654
Hue-Tam's tremendous scholarship
in this field as well

00:56:06.654 --> 00:56:08.904
in terms of thinking
about journalists' stake

00:56:11.635 --> 00:56:13.970
and radicalism as the
ways in which journalism

00:56:13.970 --> 00:56:16.940
and the different types
of kind of print forms

00:56:16.940 --> 00:56:20.580
allow for these types
of political organizing

00:56:20.580 --> 00:56:22.943
as well as forms of literary expression.

00:56:23.830 --> 00:56:27.593
Yet I think what is interesting about the,

00:56:28.621 --> 00:56:31.540
kind of the libraries
in this larger ecosystem

00:56:31.540 --> 00:56:35.840
is that it has, so there's

00:56:36.703 --> 00:56:38.320
because of the ways in which
the libraries was organized,

00:56:38.320 --> 00:56:41.303
of course, it was funded
by the governmental state.

00:56:42.241 --> 00:56:46.290
It had this deeply archival
preservation mentality

00:56:46.290 --> 00:56:50.900
of both collecting what was
considered like works of value

00:56:50.900 --> 00:56:53.500
both based on a French notion of value.

00:56:53.500 --> 00:56:57.710
And then also there was
a preservation mentality

00:56:57.710 --> 00:57:00.990
where not only was the library operated

00:57:00.990 --> 00:57:03.640
as the archives and as
these public libraries,

00:57:03.640 --> 00:57:06.790
but it also operated as the legal deposit.

00:57:06.790 --> 00:57:09.100
So that means that in principle,

00:57:09.100 --> 00:57:12.750
every work that was officially
filed for publication

00:57:12.750 --> 00:57:16.310
in Indochina, one copy needed to be filed

00:57:16.310 --> 00:57:18.520
in the regional library.

00:57:18.520 --> 00:57:20.961
So if something is published in Saigon,

00:57:20.961 --> 00:57:22.145
one copy needed to be filed

00:57:22.145 --> 00:57:24.403
in the Saigon Cochinchina library,

00:57:24.403 --> 00:57:25.870
and then one copy needed to be submitted

00:57:26.864 --> 00:57:28.360
to the Hanoi Central
Library into the Book Depot.

00:57:28.360 --> 00:57:30.630
And then one other copy
needed to be submitted

00:57:30.630 --> 00:57:34.150
to the legal deposit in the
National Library in Paris.

00:57:34.150 --> 00:57:36.856
And this is where with John McHale's work

00:57:36.856 --> 00:57:38.782
where he analyzed a lot of
the legal deposit works,

00:57:38.782 --> 00:57:39.780
that's how those collections were built.

00:57:39.780 --> 00:57:43.480
So in principle that
reflects a certain slice

00:57:45.620 --> 00:57:48.200
of what was the print,
reading, production,

00:57:48.200 --> 00:57:51.610
literary, journalistic, print production

00:57:51.610 --> 00:57:54.423
from the 20s to the 40s during Vietnam.

00:57:55.760 --> 00:57:58.490
And McHale has pointed
to like how it showcases

00:57:59.996 --> 00:58:03.210
a lot of like Buddhist's
writing material, for example,

00:58:03.210 --> 00:58:07.907
as well as kind of other
forms of literary genres,

00:58:09.100 --> 00:58:14.100
satire, and then satire
as well as like fiction

00:58:14.314 --> 00:58:15.430
and things like that.

00:58:15.430 --> 00:58:17.640
However, what's interesting when you look

00:58:17.640 --> 00:58:20.534
about the materials
that are in the library,

00:58:20.534 --> 00:58:22.620
so you have this like these books

00:58:22.620 --> 00:58:24.970
of which are considered
quote unquote classics,

00:58:26.014 --> 00:58:27.657
and then you have these new forms

00:58:27.657 --> 00:58:29.590
of Indochina published materials,

00:58:29.590 --> 00:58:33.260
new as in things that are
contemporarily printed

00:58:33.260 --> 00:58:35.791
rather than retroactively preserved,

00:58:35.791 --> 00:58:37.710
and how they differ

00:58:37.710 --> 00:58:40.793
from what is basically
sold on the streets.

00:58:41.710 --> 00:58:46.710
And it's not always a blur,
like a distinct separation

00:58:47.410 --> 00:58:49.610
because some of the most
popular works as I was saying

00:58:49.610 --> 00:58:52.357
make their way into the library too

00:58:52.357 --> 00:58:53.500
because there's just
so many copies of them.

00:58:53.500 --> 00:58:56.210
They end up getting added into the library

00:58:56.210 --> 00:58:57.593
because of its popularity.

00:58:58.534 --> 00:59:00.170
However, there was still,

00:59:00.170 --> 00:59:02.560
library acquisitions still
went through a process

00:59:02.560 --> 00:59:03.513
of somewhat,

00:59:06.546 --> 00:59:09.714
of indirect censorship,

00:59:09.714 --> 00:59:12.310
which is that those of
which was not seen as

00:59:12.310 --> 00:59:15.230
well, as seen as more
political or radicalizing

00:59:15.230 --> 00:59:17.278
didn't end up in the library.

00:59:17.278 --> 00:59:18.820
So it's the other material that ended up

00:59:18.820 --> 00:59:23.820
kind of circulating in
bookstores and being retranslated

00:59:26.447 --> 00:59:28.860
or rewritten and republished
in different editions.

00:59:28.860 --> 00:59:31.440
What's interesting is I have lists

00:59:32.490 --> 00:59:33.973
of things of which are books,

00:59:35.655 --> 00:59:37.896
of which kind of accidentally
end up in the library

00:59:37.896 --> 00:59:39.336
of which shouldn't have been there

00:59:39.336 --> 00:59:41.333
which is really interesting because then

00:59:41.333 --> 00:59:43.196
it kind of points to that

00:59:43.196 --> 00:59:44.930
this regime of control was much weaker

00:59:44.930 --> 00:59:47.793
than we anticipate,
which is often the case.

00:59:48.914 --> 00:59:51.460
So for example, like
Nguyễn An Ninh translated

00:59:51.460 --> 00:59:53.117
Rousseau's "Social Contract."

00:59:53.971 --> 00:59:55.580
And that circulated in the
library for quite some time.

00:59:55.580 --> 00:59:59.480
And it wasn't, it was
debated as to like how risky

00:59:59.480 --> 01:00:01.070
or dangerous these works were.

01:00:01.070 --> 01:00:02.980
But then it kind of, when it was found,

01:00:02.980 --> 01:00:05.710
it was immediately pulled
from the library, for example.

01:00:05.710 --> 01:00:08.370
Or there's other like biographies
of Chinese nationalists.

01:00:08.370 --> 01:00:11.130
So I think those are like,
that's a quintessential example

01:00:11.130 --> 01:00:14.750
of the illicit reading
of which is circulating.

01:00:14.750 --> 01:00:18.690
And that also ended up in the library

01:00:18.690 --> 01:00:21.163
and then immediately got flagged as well.

01:00:22.113 --> 01:00:24.090
There were also initiatives to
start like smaller libraries

01:00:24.090 --> 01:00:26.780
and to officially open a library

01:00:26.780 --> 01:00:30.990
and have it recognized by the state,

01:00:30.990 --> 01:00:33.730
you had to publicly post what the library,

01:00:33.730 --> 01:00:35.818
even if it was like an association,

01:00:35.818 --> 01:00:38.175
you had to publicly post
what materials were in there.

01:00:38.175 --> 01:00:41.330
And often, so then the colonial state

01:00:42.171 --> 01:00:43.817
was trying to police these cases

01:00:43.817 --> 01:00:45.833
of like these illicit
libraries that are opening

01:00:45.833 --> 01:00:47.471
without official registration.

01:00:47.471 --> 01:00:50.390
And then it's often lists of these,

01:00:50.390 --> 01:00:53.510
either like biographies of revolutionaries

01:00:53.510 --> 01:00:57.103
that are circulating, a
lot of political tracts,

01:00:58.060 --> 01:01:01.337
criticisms, explicit
criticisms of the state.

01:01:01.337 --> 01:01:06.337
I think pretty like overtly
anti-colonial texts.

01:01:07.235 --> 01:01:08.600
And those libraries nearly tried to,

01:01:08.600 --> 01:01:10.223
were shut down or policed.

01:01:13.597 --> 01:01:14.430
I realize there's so many questions,

01:01:14.430 --> 01:01:17.794
and I hope is there a way that
I could also save the chat?

01:01:17.794 --> 01:01:21.457
So feel free to please also
throw things in the chat.

01:01:21.457 --> 01:01:22.574
- There are some questions in the chat,

01:01:22.574 --> 01:01:24.800
and then let's see in the participants,

01:01:24.800 --> 01:01:27.790
Al Lin has also raised his hand.

01:01:27.790 --> 01:01:28.623
- Oh, okay.

01:01:28.623 --> 01:01:29.794
- [Quan] Go ahead.

01:01:29.794 --> 01:01:31.632
- Hi, thank you so much for this talk.

01:01:31.632 --> 01:01:33.489
It's super, super
fascinating to hear about.

01:01:33.489 --> 01:01:36.293
I'm thinking a lot about
Anderson and national identity.

01:01:36.293 --> 01:01:38.934
I love the kind of quote
from Viet's article

01:01:38.934 --> 01:01:40.392
talking about civility and the connection

01:01:40.392 --> 01:01:41.490
