01:01:41.490 --> 01:01:43.300
I mean, I even think that print culture

01:01:43.300 --> 01:01:45.370
is that alternative to print capitalism

01:01:45.370 --> 01:01:47.103
that Chatterjee kind of critiques.

01:01:48.027 --> 01:01:49.610
And so thinking about print
culture instead of capitalism

01:01:49.610 --> 01:01:51.810
as a much more nuanced way to think about

01:01:51.810 --> 01:01:54.090
kind of like national
identity and formation,

01:01:54.090 --> 01:01:56.580
and I'm wondering how
successful were libraries

01:01:56.580 --> 01:01:58.963
as part of articulating national identity?

01:01:59.886 --> 01:02:02.226
So in so much that they do,

01:02:02.226 --> 01:02:03.526
how do we measure its success?

01:02:03.526 --> 01:02:05.280
Especially I guess with
the decolonization chapter,

01:02:05.280 --> 01:02:06.530
and one way to think about it.

01:02:06.530 --> 01:02:08.120
I love that you mentioned
that Laos, for example,

01:02:08.120 --> 01:02:10.028
didn't have a library.

01:02:10.028 --> 01:02:11.780
So how did they, for example,
articulate national identity

01:02:11.780 --> 01:02:16.237
as part of, without a kind
of equivalent library?

01:02:16.237 --> 01:02:19.246
And then also thinking
about how folks are like

01:02:19.246 --> 01:02:21.144
colonial authorities
have different agendas.

01:02:21.144 --> 01:02:23.030
So they're trying to like articulate

01:02:23.030 --> 01:02:25.585
a kind of forceful kind of like a position

01:02:25.585 --> 01:02:27.187
of certain things versus like borrowers

01:02:27.187 --> 01:02:28.546
who are like borrowing books

01:02:28.546 --> 01:02:30.246
and not necessarily thinking
about national identity

01:02:30.246 --> 01:02:31.245
in that way.

01:02:31.245 --> 01:02:33.927
So it's just a lot of, I
guess, a lot of moving parts.

01:02:33.927 --> 01:02:35.601
And I think to Eric's previous question,

01:02:35.601 --> 01:02:37.210
this idea about repression
like censorship,

01:02:37.210 --> 01:02:39.590
like a Foucaultian take,
like the more you oppress,

01:02:39.590 --> 01:02:40.750
that doesn't really work in that way.

01:02:40.750 --> 01:02:42.627
So the more colonial people

01:02:42.627 --> 01:02:43.460
try to articulate national identity

01:02:43.460 --> 01:02:46.165
that ends up kind of working
in very different ways.

01:02:46.165 --> 01:02:48.000
So I'm just wondering how do we track kind

01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:49.909
of that relationship between, yeah,

01:02:49.909 --> 01:02:51.283
nationalism and libraries?

