01:03:30.830 --> 01:03:33.380
about like what makes the
library kind of different

01:03:33.380 --> 01:03:36.406
in this larger ecosystem of other books?

01:03:36.406 --> 01:03:39.620
Is that the story of
the library in Indochina

01:03:39.620 --> 01:03:41.406
is also the story,

01:03:41.406 --> 01:03:44.300
like it has, I would say two
other intertwined stories,

01:03:44.300 --> 01:03:47.640
which is one on education
and two on language.

01:03:47.640 --> 01:03:50.580
And I would argue that

01:03:50.580 --> 01:03:53.320
especially thinking about decolonization

01:03:53.320 --> 01:03:55.770
and like let's take the specific instance

01:03:55.770 --> 01:03:59.020
of the Cochinchina Library

01:03:59.020 --> 01:04:03.307
of which underwent extensive
processes of decolonization

01:04:03.307 --> 01:04:06.630
in the 50s, and then got in 54 to 55

01:04:06.630 --> 01:04:10.470
with the division of
North and South Vietnam,

01:04:10.470 --> 01:04:13.889
the Saigon or the Cochinchina
Library inherited a third

01:04:13.889 --> 01:04:17.240
of the French colonial
collections in Hanoi,

01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:20.380
as well as all the staff
and the University of Hanoi.

01:04:20.380 --> 01:04:24.502
So there were all these
colonial institutions

01:04:24.502 --> 01:04:25.990
that then were physically, logistically

01:04:25.990 --> 01:04:28.030
moved down to Saigon.

01:04:28.030 --> 01:04:31.629
And then Saigon, the Cochinchina Library,

01:04:31.629 --> 01:04:32.590
this tiny, already cramped library

01:04:32.590 --> 01:04:34.747
inherited this collection.

01:04:34.747 --> 01:04:36.490
And it wasn't just like a physical merger,

01:04:36.490 --> 01:04:39.820
but from the fifth,
actually really earlier

01:04:39.820 --> 01:04:43.780
from like 45 to, and
especially through 49,

01:04:43.780 --> 01:04:45.468
the Cochinchina Library

01:04:45.468 --> 01:04:46.630
as it was going through this
process of de-colonization

01:04:46.630 --> 01:04:50.580
is trying to articulate how
do we distinguish ourselves

01:04:50.580 --> 01:04:51.920
from our colonial past

01:04:51.920 --> 01:04:55.220
but also we are inheriting all
of these colonial vestiges,

01:04:55.220 --> 01:04:57.120
French language materials, for example,

01:04:57.120 --> 01:04:59.090
a lot of materials about in the archives

01:04:59.090 --> 01:05:00.770
about the colonial state.

01:05:00.770 --> 01:05:05.030
So how do we distinguish
ourselves as a Vietnamese nation,

01:05:05.030 --> 01:05:07.330
and this process as we understand through

01:05:07.330 --> 01:05:10.407
studying the Republic of Vietnam

01:05:10.407 --> 01:05:12.647
and also through the war was constantly

01:05:12.647 --> 01:05:14.590
trying to distinguish itself
from North Vietnam too

01:05:14.590 --> 01:05:17.947
which is at, the South Vietnam and Saigon

01:05:17.947 --> 01:05:20.623
was trying to articulate itself as this,

01:05:21.540 --> 01:05:25.709
as this national cultural
heritage preserver

01:05:25.709 --> 01:05:28.327
of the legitimate form of
all Vietnamese identity.

01:05:28.327 --> 01:05:29.240
And that, and this is where I argue

01:05:29.240 --> 01:05:32.480
that the library played
a very key part in this.

01:05:32.480 --> 01:05:35.950
So because it inherited all
these colonial materials,

01:05:35.950 --> 01:05:39.060
it also sought and advocated

01:05:39.060 --> 01:05:41.347
that this was part of its mission

01:05:41.347 --> 01:05:42.910
was that we are preserving
this long history

01:05:42.910 --> 01:05:45.430
of all of Vietnamese history,

01:05:45.430 --> 01:05:49.650
kind of symbolized through
inheriting this collection,

01:05:49.650 --> 01:05:53.890
inheriting actually some of
the precolonial collections

01:05:53.890 --> 01:05:56.360
that were also preserved in Hanoi.

01:05:56.360 --> 01:05:59.290
And then additionally,
looking into the present

01:05:59.290 --> 01:06:01.520
and to the future,
which is that we needed,

01:06:01.520 --> 01:06:04.340
as a library you needed to advocate

01:06:04.340 --> 01:06:06.260
and transform language acquisition

01:06:06.260 --> 01:06:09.758
or like the acquisition
of materials in Vietnamese

01:06:09.758 --> 01:06:10.879
rather than in French.

01:06:10.879 --> 01:06:12.555
So there was, there was a huge shift

01:06:12.555 --> 01:06:16.100
to nationalize the library
through the collection

01:06:16.100 --> 01:06:18.710
of adamantly searching out

01:06:18.710 --> 01:06:22.040
and adding all Vietnamese
materials that were published,

01:06:22.040 --> 01:06:24.133
newly published in the 50's and 60's,

01:06:25.095 --> 01:06:26.694
as well as building collections

01:06:26.694 --> 01:06:27.773
like Chinese language collections

01:06:27.773 --> 01:06:30.377
as well as part of the Saigon library.

01:06:30.377 --> 01:06:33.714
And this is where kind of
going back to your question

01:06:33.714 --> 01:06:36.055
of how does one articulate
this national identity,

01:06:36.055 --> 01:06:37.710
I would argue is it's
very closely intertwined

01:06:37.710 --> 01:06:42.313
with these language policies
as well as the library,

01:06:45.330 --> 01:06:47.570
especially this case
of the Saigon Library,

01:06:47.570 --> 01:06:51.073
symbolically functioning as this fortress,

01:06:52.205 --> 01:06:55.220
like I think the quote
said a fortress of culture.

01:06:55.220 --> 01:06:57.540
And a fortress of culture and heritage

01:06:57.540 --> 01:07:00.780
that really articulated
a national identity

01:07:00.780 --> 01:07:04.393
kind of looking forward but
also looking to its past, right?

01:07:05.365 --> 01:07:07.523
So looking to preserve and create

01:07:07.523 --> 01:07:10.460
this kind of streamlined understanding

01:07:10.460 --> 01:07:12.420
of what was considered

01:07:12.420 --> 01:07:15.173
legitimate Vietnamese national identity.

01:07:16.800 --> 01:07:19.040
I think I'll stop there because
I can keep going with this

01:07:19.040 --> 01:07:21.637
because it's such a
fascinating way of framing.

01:07:21.637 --> 01:07:23.503
And thank you so much for that question.

01:07:26.222 --> 01:07:28.886
- All right, we have
Michelle Thompson next

01:07:28.886 --> 01:07:30.886
and then after Michelle, Dr. Norton Yan.

01:07:32.962 --> 01:07:34.287
- Hi, this was just
really a wonderful talk,

01:07:34.287 --> 01:07:36.800
and I was very happy to
see it on the schedule

01:07:36.800 --> 01:07:39.560
because it kind of connects
with some work I did earlier

01:07:39.560 --> 01:07:43.103
on what gets put into an
archive and what does not.

01:07:43.103 --> 01:07:44.400
I was focusing mainly on no.

01:07:44.400 --> 01:07:46.864
I originally had several
questions for you,

01:07:46.864 --> 01:07:48.281
but you've kind of addressed some of them

01:07:48.281 --> 01:07:49.490
in the question and answer period.

01:07:49.490 --> 01:07:51.700
But one thing that hasn't been touched on,

01:07:51.700 --> 01:07:55.444
but just was just barely
brought up in your answer

01:07:55.444 --> 01:07:58.150
just a minute ago is connected to staff.

01:07:58.150 --> 01:08:01.940
And there's a wonderful
novel I read a few years ago,

01:08:01.940 --> 01:08:04.200
and I'm trying to remember
the exact name of it,

01:08:04.200 --> 01:08:06.980
but it's about a woman
in the early 20th century

01:08:06.980 --> 01:08:08.540
in the United States who fought

01:08:09.487 --> 01:08:11.620
to get to be allowed to
be a part of the staff

01:08:11.620 --> 01:08:14.080
of a library in the Appalachian mountains

01:08:14.080 --> 01:08:17.260
who was delivering books
to people on horseback.

01:08:17.260 --> 01:08:20.104
She literally had a
route like a mail route

01:08:20.104 --> 01:08:22.722
that she covered like once
every couple of weeks or so.

01:08:22.722 --> 01:08:24.905
It's a really great novel, but you know,

01:08:24.905 --> 01:08:26.730
I thought I would just like
to hear you talk a little bit

01:08:26.730 --> 01:08:28.701
about the staff.

01:08:28.701 --> 01:08:29.705
I mean, we don't,

01:08:29.705 --> 01:08:30.710
you didn't really say anything about say

01:08:30.710 --> 01:08:34.990
gender makeup of the staff
or, you know, effects on them,

01:08:34.990 --> 01:08:38.663
effects of them on their
own communities, et cetera.

01:08:39.545 --> 01:08:40.822
So I'd like to hear you talk a little bit

01:08:40.822 --> 01:08:42.900
about the staff of these libraries.

01:08:42.900 --> 01:08:44.070
- Thank you so much.

01:08:44.070 --> 01:08:45.680
I'm so glad you brought this question up.

01:08:45.680 --> 01:08:47.280
This was like the subject of my,

01:08:48.142 --> 01:08:50.640
one of my other talks where I
just talked about the people.

01:08:50.640 --> 01:08:55.640
So tracing the librarians and the staff

01:08:57.170 --> 01:09:01.325
was a very complicated endeavor

01:09:01.325 --> 01:09:04.450
because there was frequent
turnover in low-level staff.

01:09:04.450 --> 01:09:08.180
And this would include
from secretaries, copyists,

01:09:08.180 --> 01:09:12.730
typists, groundskeepers, those
who are maintaining security

01:09:12.730 --> 01:09:14.710
and operations of the building.

01:09:14.710 --> 01:09:16.500
But I think what's fascinating about

01:09:16.500 --> 01:09:19.080
in a lot of this
discussion about the staff

01:09:19.924 --> 01:09:22.160
and the personnel and
extensive record keeping

01:09:22.160 --> 01:09:26.108
about the personnel is
that there was this,

01:09:26.108 --> 01:09:31.108
there was this kind of vibrant world

01:09:31.810 --> 01:09:34.910
of librarian low-level staff who were,

01:09:34.910 --> 01:09:37.960
who were the primary
readers of the library

01:09:37.960 --> 01:09:38.940
because they worked there.

01:09:38.940 --> 01:09:41.369
They checked out the books the most,

01:09:41.369 --> 01:09:43.290
and you see this, especially
in the Saigon library,

01:09:43.290 --> 01:09:47.300
and it's in these,

01:09:47.300 --> 01:09:50.720
they'll be the ones who are constantly,

01:09:50.720 --> 01:09:52.430
they'll be in the book checkout records.

01:09:52.430 --> 01:09:55.667
And then they actually, because
they worked in the library

01:09:55.667 --> 01:09:59.880
end up kind of defying the restrictions

01:09:59.880 --> 01:10:01.760
of like only checking
out two books at a time,

01:10:01.760 --> 01:10:03.523
to check out four for example.

01:10:04.548 --> 01:10:08.340
And then in one of my
chapters in the 1930s,

01:10:08.340 --> 01:10:11.680
there's an extensive discussion
about how high level staff

01:10:11.680 --> 01:10:15.930
which are primarily French-trained,

01:10:15.930 --> 01:10:16.780
so either at Chartres,

01:10:16.780 --> 01:10:20.060
like that's at the highest
level of the administrators

01:10:20.060 --> 01:10:22.428
as like the Director of like, for example,

01:10:22.428 --> 01:10:25.550
the Saigon library, and then
the lower level librarians

01:10:25.550 --> 01:10:28.980
either that are French,
like racially French,

01:10:28.980 --> 01:10:31.540
end up coming into the library,

01:10:31.540 --> 01:10:33.730
either working from like

01:10:33.730 --> 01:10:35.830
other administrative governmental offices.

01:10:36.772 --> 01:10:38.073
So if they're not officially
trained in library sciences

01:10:38.073 --> 01:10:41.423
then they are trained in
other governmental work,

01:10:42.350 --> 01:10:46.593
and then you have
Vietnamese librarian staff

01:10:47.940 --> 01:10:52.811
who either have come in
because they've, I think in,

01:10:52.811 --> 01:10:54.830
like Khuong Viet technically
worked in the Saigon library

01:10:54.830 --> 01:10:59.830
before, and he actually
was very much involved,

01:10:59.890 --> 01:11:02.370
I think, Hue-Tam pointed to this too,

01:11:02.370 --> 01:11:05.000
but involved in other literacy societies

01:11:05.000 --> 01:11:07.990
in Quốc Ngữ, popularization societies.

01:11:07.990 --> 01:11:12.990
Some are involved as like
self-taught linguists.

01:11:13.150 --> 01:11:15.630
They're, they have like
a scholar bureaucrat

01:11:15.630 --> 01:11:16.920
type of identity.

01:11:16.920 --> 01:11:19.351
Oh, but then the discussion
I was talking about

01:11:19.351 --> 01:11:20.449
was like in the 30's,

01:11:20.449 --> 01:11:22.867
there was this rampant
extensive discussion between

01:11:22.867 --> 01:11:24.971
tension between high-level
staff and low-level staff

01:11:24.971 --> 01:11:27.650
because there were often
accusations against low-level staff

01:11:27.650 --> 01:11:30.010
for stealing books because
they have the most access

01:11:30.010 --> 01:11:31.943
to the library collections.

01:11:32.871 --> 01:11:34.480
So they, the low-level
staff were often seen

01:11:34.480 --> 01:11:36.553
as suspect from high-level staff.

01:11:37.407 --> 01:11:42.407
And, yeah, so and then were dismissed

01:11:43.240 --> 01:11:48.240
because of other tensions or kind of,

01:11:52.447 --> 01:11:53.280
how would I describe this,

01:11:53.280 --> 01:11:56.160
as kind of disconnects between
different working cultures?

01:11:56.160 --> 01:11:58.060
So for example, people showing up late

01:11:58.060 --> 01:12:01.710
or people defying the library rules,

01:12:01.710 --> 01:12:04.000
coming on weekends,
coming when it's closed.

01:12:04.000 --> 01:12:07.098
So using this space in
ways that were intended.

01:12:07.098 --> 01:12:09.745
So these are library staff
like living in this space,

01:12:09.745 --> 01:12:11.840
like after hours, not leaving,

01:12:11.840 --> 01:12:16.060
bringing in their friends,
like things of which were seen

01:12:16.060 --> 01:12:19.030
as defying a very kind of rigid policy

01:12:19.030 --> 01:12:20.640
of library preservation

01:12:21.825 --> 01:12:25.040
or kind of not being as attentive

01:12:25.040 --> 01:12:27.603
to like recording checkouts, for example.

01:12:28.449 --> 01:12:29.282
So I have a lot of these,

01:12:29.282 --> 01:12:33.550
what I see as these everyday
dramas of library staff

01:12:33.550 --> 01:12:37.330
amongst each other, but
also tensions between,

01:12:37.330 --> 01:12:40.220
like I have a case for
example, of a French reader

01:12:40.220 --> 01:12:41.710
in the Hanoi central library

01:12:42.868 --> 01:12:47.650
who basically physically attacked

01:12:47.650 --> 01:12:50.589
a low-level Vietnamese librarian

01:12:50.589 --> 01:12:53.050
because that librarian would
not bundle up his books

01:12:53.050 --> 01:12:54.880
that he checked out.

01:12:54.880 --> 01:12:56.663
So then the French administrator,

01:12:57.828 --> 01:13:01.740
or the French reader patron
punched the librarian,

01:13:01.740 --> 01:13:05.066
and then the librarian staff
ended up like reporting this

01:13:05.066 --> 01:13:05.899
to a higher administration.

01:13:05.899 --> 01:13:09.550
But then there was no kind
of type of like consequences

01:13:09.550 --> 01:13:11.813
for these, this type of behavior.

01:13:12.764 --> 01:13:14.920
And then that really points
to the racial hierarchies

01:13:14.920 --> 01:13:18.980
within reading of these, of the libraries.

01:13:18.980 --> 01:13:21.625
So I have so many of
these stories, Michelle,

01:13:21.625 --> 01:13:23.468
I think it's just,

01:13:23.468 --> 01:13:25.766
it really I'm trying to
still make sense of them

01:13:25.766 --> 01:13:30.766
just because it, they're
are so nuanced and textured.

01:13:31.550 --> 01:13:33.430
Oh, and then the other one
I wanted to mention also

01:13:33.430 --> 01:13:38.350
is gender, is that the
majority of the library staff

01:13:38.350 --> 01:13:43.350
were male, and low-level were
definitely racially Vietnamese

01:13:44.964 --> 01:13:47.240
but then often the discussion

01:13:47.240 --> 01:13:49.320
about hiring Vietnamese librarian

01:13:49.320 --> 01:13:51.430
or sorry female librarian staff

01:13:52.667 --> 01:13:56.983
was seen as a question also of,

01:13:57.899 --> 01:14:00.800
it was race and gender
questions because it costs more

01:14:00.800 --> 01:14:04.861
to hire a female, a
French female librarian,

01:14:04.861 --> 01:14:06.500
even though, and then a lot
of French female librarians

01:14:06.500 --> 01:14:08.300
ended up being secretaries,

01:14:08.300 --> 01:14:11.320
but then over time because of the costs

01:14:11.320 --> 01:14:14.430
of paying French administrators

01:14:14.430 --> 01:14:16.610
or French library administrators

01:14:16.610 --> 01:14:21.065
there is a shift to train more
Vietnamese administrators.

01:14:21.065 --> 01:14:25.448
And then those were,
ended up often being male.

01:14:25.448 --> 01:14:28.430
I think I'll end there

01:14:28.430 --> 01:14:30.800
because I could go into another story

01:14:30.800 --> 01:14:34.690
about Suzanne Karpeles,
but I think I'll stop here.

01:14:36.405 --> 01:14:38.960
- Okay, and we'll have time
for maybe one last question

01:14:38.960 --> 01:14:40.924
from Dr. Norton Yan.

01:14:40.924 --> 01:14:43.084
And if you still have
questions for Cindy Nguyen,

01:14:43.084 --> 01:14:43.917
please drop them in the chat,

01:14:43.917 --> 01:14:45.670
and we'll share those with her later.

01:14:49.563 --> 01:14:51.993
- This is a question
that related to Michelle,

01:14:53.383 --> 01:14:54.533
because classification,

01:14:55.765 --> 01:14:57.721
there are a lot of classification

01:14:57.721 --> 01:15:00.471
in the library and some of
them are pre-classification.

01:15:02.220 --> 01:15:03.853
That means acquisition.

01:15:05.526 --> 01:15:08.990
And then the classification
in Vietnam is very complex

01:15:08.990 --> 01:15:11.987
through the Russian system.

01:15:11.987 --> 01:15:15.243
And then through the American system,

01:15:15.243 --> 01:15:17.124
the Dewey system and MARC 21.

01:15:17.124 --> 01:15:22.124
I am doing a lot of village documentation.

01:15:23.950 --> 01:15:28.950
And then it will be like
to bypass all of that.

01:15:29.547 --> 01:15:31.985
And then to go directly into a computer.

01:15:31.985 --> 01:15:36.717
To library online is
probably have to go to

01:15:38.748 --> 01:15:42.533
along with a library organization,

01:15:43.400 --> 01:15:47.664
so that they don't have to fire staff.

01:15:47.664 --> 01:15:51.443
And then if staff feeling that they fired,

01:15:51.443 --> 01:15:53.093
then they would resist on change.

01:15:54.630 --> 01:15:56.123
That is one of my questions,

01:15:57.028 --> 01:15:59.810
and I hope to know that
what kind of classification

01:15:59.810 --> 01:16:03.253
were used in the libraries
in Vietnam and Cambodia.

01:16:05.130 --> 01:16:07.830
- Thank you so much for this question.

01:16:07.830 --> 01:16:09.460
Definitely you're pointing to

01:16:12.663 --> 01:16:14.330
the complex information
systems that were used

01:16:14.330 --> 01:16:15.783
over the different processes,

01:16:16.763 --> 01:16:19.545
over the colonial and
the post-colonial time.

01:16:19.545 --> 01:16:20.890
So during the colonial period,

01:16:20.890 --> 01:16:23.684
they followed the Bibliothèque nationale,

01:16:23.684 --> 01:16:26.145
the French model of classifications,

01:16:26.145 --> 01:16:31.145
and then this was thematically
organized as well as,

01:16:33.220 --> 01:16:34.760
so there were two catalogs that were used,

01:16:34.760 --> 01:16:37.602
one by theme by topic,
and then one by author.

01:16:37.602 --> 01:16:40.520
And then these cataloging systems

01:16:41.576 --> 01:16:43.326
as the libraries continued to grow,

01:16:44.466 --> 01:16:46.810
it wasn't always updated.

01:16:46.810 --> 01:16:49.981
So like the author catalog for example

01:16:49.981 --> 01:16:51.720
was definitely not updated as much,

01:16:51.720 --> 01:16:53.360
and it kind of fell apart.

01:16:53.360 --> 01:16:57.202
So often the classification system

01:16:57.202 --> 01:16:58.340
during the colonial period

01:16:59.826 --> 01:17:00.659
was in principal modeled that way.

01:17:00.659 --> 01:17:03.253
But then in practice, a lot of the,

01:17:05.124 --> 01:17:08.023
there were also other aspects
of the library reading room

01:17:08.023 --> 01:17:09.700
where there were books of
which were on the shelves

01:17:09.700 --> 01:17:11.053
for free perusal.

01:17:12.086 --> 01:17:14.025
So often these were like
more like reference,

01:17:14.025 --> 01:17:16.886
encyclopedia as well as
contemporary periodicals

01:17:16.886 --> 01:17:18.286
that were available.

01:17:18.286 --> 01:17:20.103
And then there were
separate lending sections

01:17:20.103 --> 01:17:22.123
which operated their own kind of,

01:17:22.123 --> 01:17:23.760
and these are collections
of about 10,000 works,

01:17:23.760 --> 01:17:27.650
and these would operate with a theme

01:17:27.650 --> 01:17:31.833
or a topic-oriented catalog
and then author catalog.

01:17:33.260 --> 01:17:35.483
What's interesting is by the 50's,

01:17:36.680 --> 01:17:39.310
there was an attempt to implement
the Dewey decimal system

01:17:39.310 --> 01:17:43.350
as well, as well as to
put the Saigon library

01:17:43.350 --> 01:17:46.630
more within kind of
international library standards.

01:17:46.630 --> 01:17:49.750
And then at the same time,
as you're mentioning as well,

01:17:49.750 --> 01:17:52.770
is that the DRV in Hanoi,

01:17:52.770 --> 01:17:57.083
the library then was directly
using the Russian, the BBK,

01:17:58.045 --> 01:18:00.110
the BBK classification system

01:18:01.203 --> 01:18:03.440
that followed like,

01:18:03.440 --> 01:18:05.840
well Soviet as well as socialist notions

01:18:05.840 --> 01:18:07.650
of information organizing.

01:18:07.650 --> 01:18:11.410
So I believe I haven't looked
at the classification system

01:18:11.410 --> 01:18:13.700
in a while, but how I
understand it is I think

01:18:13.700 --> 01:18:17.700
the first three are like Marx, Lenin,

01:18:17.700 --> 01:18:20.120
ways of which are like, are more about

01:18:21.003 --> 01:18:22.925
like socialist forms of
knowledge and information.

01:18:22.925 --> 01:18:24.183
Is that right?

01:18:24.183 --> 01:18:25.016
- Yeah.

01:18:25.016 --> 01:18:29.100
- Okay, so, and then,

01:18:29.100 --> 01:18:33.264
and now how I understand
it too, is that, gosh,

01:18:33.264 --> 01:18:34.720
I think the last time
the old card catalogs

01:18:34.720 --> 01:18:37.663
were still usable in the Hanoi,

01:18:37.663 --> 01:18:39.307
the national library in Hanoi,

01:18:39.307 --> 01:18:41.010
because it followed the
former cataloging system

01:18:41.010 --> 01:18:42.253
from the French period.

01:18:43.144 --> 01:18:46.950
Then there was a process
to put them into this,

01:18:46.950 --> 01:18:48.590
the socialist system.

01:18:48.590 --> 01:18:52.080
And then after that now, it's
going through another system

01:18:52.080 --> 01:18:54.585
of which it's having issues because of

01:18:54.585 --> 01:18:56.360
putting it into the catalog.

01:18:56.360 --> 01:18:59.330
But I think you're right in pointing out

01:18:59.330 --> 01:19:02.270
like the layered ways in which
these classification systems

01:19:02.270 --> 01:19:07.270
actually create this
disarray of the documentation

01:19:09.580 --> 01:19:14.490
and point to kind of also
the different organizing

01:19:14.490 --> 01:19:17.440
or intellectual processes

01:19:17.440 --> 01:19:20.950
that underlie organizing information

01:19:20.950 --> 01:19:23.953
during these different historical periods.

01:19:26.664 --> 01:19:29.903
- [Dr. Nhan] Thank you,
thank you very much.

01:19:29.903 --> 01:19:31.380
- Thank you for all of
these wonderful questions

01:19:31.380 --> 01:19:33.786
and Cindy for answering them.

01:19:33.786 --> 01:19:35.290
That will wrap it up for the day.

01:19:35.290 --> 01:19:38.223
So please join me in thanking Cindy Nguyen

01:19:39.382 --> 01:19:44.382
for this amazing and fantastic
project that she's had done.

01:19:44.561 --> 01:19:46.401
- Thank you so much.

01:19:46.401 --> 01:19:47.650
What great questions.

01:19:47.650 --> 01:19:48.483
Thank you.

